var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators-Library-Functions","page":"Library","text":"The abstract types, the weak form and the slab core with its integrator shell and its diagnostics.\n\nThe manual is split by source layer, so that no generated page grows without bound: this page holds methods.jl, core/ and diagnostics.jl; the page The ansatz families holds ansatz/; Couplings and initial guesses holds couplings/ and initial_guess/; and Example problems and diagnostics holds the problem layer, the submodules of the four example problems and the figures. The page The slab seam says how the parts of the slab integrator fit together.\n\nEach block that documents the top-level module names the source files of its layer, and Documenter matches a file by the end of its path, so a source file that no block names leaves its docstrings out of the manual, which the build reports.","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators Library Functions"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabCoupling","page":"Library","text":"SlabCoupling\n\nAbstract type of a coupling of one slab.\n\nA coupling adds the terms that the interior weak form alone does not enforce: the initial condition, the boundary conditions, or both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabCoupling"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabFitMethod","page":"Library","text":"SlabFitMethod\n\nAbstract type of a fit method of a FitTo: the way the ansatz is brought to a target. The package ships Projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabFitMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabInitialGuess","page":"Library","text":"SlabInitialGuess\n\nAbstract type of an initial guess of one slab: Continuation or FitTo.\n\nThe initial guess is one of three roles of the construction of a guess. The other two have types of their own, so that a value of the wrong role is refused where it is given: a SlabTarget is what a FitTo fits, and a SlabFitMethod is how it fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabInitialGuess"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTarget","page":"Library","text":"SlabTarget\n\nAbstract type of a target of a FitTo: the function on the slab that the ansatz is fitted to. The targets of the package are InitialCondition, PreviousSlab, ExactSolution and TargetFunction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTarget"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeAnsatz","page":"Library","text":"SpaceTimeAnsatz\n\nAbstract type of a space-time ansatz of one slab.\n\nA slab is the space-time strip that one time step spans, [t_n, t_{n+1}] × xspan. The ansatz carries it in the normalised time coordinate t̂, where it is the rectangle [0, 1] × xspan.\n\nAn ansatz discretises the field u and its derivatives v = ∂ₜu and w = ∂ₓu on the slab, and gives the integrator the basis functions and the Jacobian from which it assembles the residual. It is not a method: a SpaceTimeMethod, such as SpaceTimeGalerkin, holds one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeAnsatz"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeMethod","page":"Library","text":"SpaceTimeMethod\n\nAbstract type of a space-time method: an integrator that advances one slab per step, with an SpaceTimeAnsatz inside.\n\nIt is a GeometricMethod, so GeometricIntegrator(problem, method) accepts it, and integrate takes one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeMethod"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#GeometricBase.integrate-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, SpaceTimeMethod}","page":"Library","text":"integrate(problem::LagrangianPDEProblem, method::SpaceTimeMethod; on_failure = :warn, kwargs...)\nintegrate(integrator::GeometricIntegrator; on_failure = :warn)\n\nIntegrate problem with method and return the named tuple (; solution, traces, stats).\n\nsolution is the GeometricSolution on the output grid, and traces holds one entry per stored step: the top trace (u, v, w) of that step at the nodes of the space quadrature, as the method's top_trace gives it, or nothing where the method keeps none. The trace is needed at the quadrature nodes rather than the interior ones because the energy diagnostics integrate it there, at the accuracy of the Gauss rule.\n\nstats is the named tuple (; iterations, residuals, initial_residuals, converged) of the nonlinear solves, with one entry per stored trace, as the method's solve_stats records them, or nothing where the method keeps no record; on_failure then has nothing to act on. A step has converged when its residual is at most f_abstol + f_reltol · r₀, with r₀ its initial residual and the tolerances of the integrator's own solver options; the term in r₀ counts only for a finite r₀, so a residual that is not finite never converges. A step that has not converged warns with on_failure = :warn, the default, and throws an ErrorException with on_failure = :error; both name the step. Any other value of on_failure throws an ArgumentError before the first step.\n\nThe steps are driven one at a time, because the trace of a step exists only while its cache does. Every call starts a new run: the integrator's cache is reset first, so its first slab takes the problem's initial data and the steps it records belong to this run alone. As in the ecosystem's integrate!, a step whose state holds a NaN ends the run with a warning; traces then ends at that step, and the solution holds the NaN there. A step whose nonlinear solve raises a NonlinearSolverException ends the run with a warning too; traces then ends at the step before it, and the solution is valid up to that step only. The keyword arguments other than on_failure go to the integrator's constructor, and to nothing else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricBase.integrate"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.solve_stats-Tuple{Any}","page":"Library","text":"solve_stats(cache)\n\nThe records of the nonlinear solves of the steps taken since the last reset! of the cache, as the named tuple (; iterations, residuals, initial_residuals, converged), one entry per step.\n\nintegrate reads it after every step. A method whose cache keeps no record returns nothing, which is the default. A method that keeps one extends this function for its cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.solve_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_trace-Tuple{Any}","page":"Library","text":"top_trace(cache)\n\nThe top trace (u, v, w) of the last step, at the nodes of the space quadrature.\n\nA method whose cache keeps no trace returns nothing, which is the default. A method that keeps one extends this function, and the trace is stored beside the solution by integrate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_trace"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.CouplingData","page":"Library","text":"CouplingData\n\nThe coupling's block of the slab cache.\n\nA coupling fixes how the free coefficients of a slab are laid out, adds terms to the residual, may transform the field, and may carry unknowns of its own between the coefficients and behind them. The block carries the coupling itself, in a field coupling, and what the coupling keeps between slabs — its datum, its unknowns, the tables its residual term evaluates and the scratch of that evaluation. It is built once, when the cache is built, and its type is a parameter of the cache. Every function of the coupling that runs within a step dispatches on the block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.CouplingData"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.NoCouplingData","page":"Library","text":"NoCouplingData{C}\n\nThe block of a coupling C that keeps no data of its own: it carries the coupling alone, and has nothing to guess, to update or to copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.NoCouplingData"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeGalerkin","page":"Library","text":"SpaceTimeGalerkin{A, C, G} <: SpaceTimeMethod\n\nThe space-time Galerkin slab integrator: an ansatz A, a coupling C of consecutive slabs and an initial guess G.\n\nThe ansatz is a SpaceTimeAnsatz, the coupling a SlabCoupling and the guess a SlabInitialGuess — Continuation or FitTo. A value of another role, a method in the place of the ansatz or a target in the place of the guess, is refused when the method is built.\n\nOne step of the integrator solves one slab. The unknown of a TensorBSplineAnsatz is its coefficient array, (Nt, Nx) per component, the unknown of a SymbolicAnsatz the parameter vector of its expression, and the unknown of a NetworkAnsatz the free part of its parameter vector. For the coefficient array, a coupling that fixes the bottom row of the time direction supplies it as a datum — the initial condition at the first step and the previous slab's top row after it — and the remaining Nt - 1 rows are determined by the interior weak Euler–Lagrange form of the slab, tested against the basis functions that vanish at the top of the slab. The v term of that form leaves one boundary term at the bottom of the slab, the momentum flux, which the coupling supplies as well; the top of the slab is free, so its flux is not imposed but measured, and handed on as the next slab's bottom flux.\n\nThe unknown count is the coupling's: the free rows of each space function and each component, plus the coupling's own unknowns behind them. A CoefficientRestriction fixes the bottom row, so it leaves (Nt - 1) * Nx * D free coefficients; a weak-multiplier coupling imposes the initial condition with multipliers of its own, so all Nt rows are free and the multipliers follow them. A symbolic ansatz has no rows, and neither has a network: their parameters are their free unknowns, and WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions, the one coupling either takes, puts its multipliers after them. A step is a nonlinear system of total_free_coefficients(method, problem) equations, which the ecosystem's Newton solver solves, with a pivoted QR as its linear solve for a symbolic or a network ansatz. With linear_solver = true, for an ansatz whose step is affine in its unknowns and a linear density, one linear solve replaces Newton's iterations: the step's equations are the slab's weak-form rows — a network's interior weak form beside the coupling's weak initial and boundary rows — with the Jacobian of the whole system, and a solution is read from zero and then refined once with the same factorisation, two solves in all and no iterate. The factorisation is the solver's own linear method — the linear_solver_method of its configuration, which is SimpleSolvers.LapackPivotedQR for a network — taken at the run's first step and again at each step whose guess has moved the parameters the step holds fixed, because the Jacobian depends on them: for a :last_layer network those are the hidden parameters before its free output weights, of which the cache keeps a copy and compares it exactly. The Jacobian of such a step is a saddle point whose constraint rows reach only the free output weights of a :last_layer network, so the pivoted QR of a network solves it by its minimum-norm step, where a factorisation without rank detection raises on a zero pivot. In Float32 that step leaves a residual of order 1e-2 of the initial one — at most 2.4e-2 over three steps of LinearTransport at S = 20 and D = 2, where Float64 leaves 1.5e-14 — so the solver's default tolerances record such a step as not converged. The iterate count, the residual, the initial residual and the convergence of every step are recorded in the cache, and the trace (u, v, w) at the top of the slab, at the nodes of the space quadrature, is kept for the diagnostics.\n\nThe roles of the ansatz, the coupling and the guess, and the functions through which the core reaches each of them, are on the page The slab seam of the manual.\n\nmethod = SpaceTimeGalerkin(\n    TensorBSplineAnsatz(degree = 2, ncells = (1, 20), xspan = (0.0, 1.0)),\n    CoefficientRestriction();\n    initial_guess = Continuation(),\n    linear_solver = false)\n\nresult = integrate(problem, method)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeGalerkin"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeGalerkinCache","page":"Library","text":"SpaceTimeGalerkinCache{ST, AD, CD}\n\nThe cache of the slab integrator.\n\nx is the nonlinear solver's vector: the free coefficients of the slab, laid out component-major with the time index fastest — the entry of free row a ∈ 1:Nf, space function j and component d is a + (j - 1) * Nf + (d - 1) * Nf * Nx, with Nf the coupling's free-row count — and then the coupling's own unknowns, in the layout of the coupling's block.\n\nu, v and w hold the field and its derivatives at the points of the slab's quadrature, and du, dv and dw the density's derivatives there; trace_u, trace_v and trace_w hold the trace at the top of the last slab. iterations, residuals, initial_residuals and converged record the Newton solve of every step: its iterate count, its final residual ‖F(x)‖₂, its initial residual r₀ = ‖F(x₀)‖₂ and whether the step converged. The direct linear solve records its two solves, the first and the refinement step, as two iterates. steps counts the steps taken, which is what tells the coupling and the guess whether the slab is the run's first.\n\nansatz is the ansatz's block, of type AD, which holds what the ansatz keeps of a slab, and coupling the coupling's block, of type CD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SpaceTimeGalerkinCache"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#GeometricBase.reset!-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache}","page":"Library","text":"reset!(cache::SpaceTimeGalerkinCache)\n\nClear the state a run leaves in the cache: the step counter, which is what tells the coupling and the guess whether the slab is the run's first, the counter of linear-solver factorisations, at zero of which the next step factorises again, and the per-step records of the Newton solve.\n\nA run starts at the problem's initial data, so a cache that still counted the previous run's steps would hand that run's last top row to the new first slab as its bottom datum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricBase.reset!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#GeometricBase.update!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractVector{ST}, GeometricIntegratorsBase.GeometricIntegrator{var\"#s176\", var\"#s175\", CT} where {var\"#s176\"<:SpaceTimeGalerkin, var\"#s175\"<:LagrangianPDEProblem, CT<:GeometricIntegratorsBase.CacheDict{var\"#s175\", var\"#s176\"}}}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"update!(sol, params, x, int)\n\nWrite the solution of the slab into sol, keep its trace, and let the coupling read what it hands on to the next step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricBase.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#GeometricIntegratorsBase.integrate_step!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, GeometricIntegratorsBase.GeometricIntegrator{var\"#s176\", var\"#s175\", CT} where {var\"#s176\"<:SpaceTimeGalerkin, var\"#s175\"<:LagrangianPDEProblem, CT<:GeometricIntegratorsBase.CacheDict{var\"#s175\", var\"#s176\"}}}","page":"Library","text":"integrate_step!(sol, history, params, int)\n\nOne slab: the coupling fixes its datum, the initial guess fills the solver's vector, the ecosystem's Newton solver (or, when method.linear_solver is true, one solve with the cached factorisation of the solver's own linear method, from zero, and one step of iterative refinement with it) solves the slab, the cache records the solve, and the update writes the solution, the trace and the data the next slab starts from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricIntegratorsBase.integrate_step!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#GeometricIntegratorsBase.internal_variables-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkin, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Library","text":"internal_variables(method::SpaceTimeGalerkin, problem)\n\nThe coupling's own state of a step, which the ecosystem's copy_internal_variables! fills after every step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricIntegratorsBase.internal_variables"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#GeometricIntegratorsBase.residual!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractVector{ST}, AbstractVector{ST}, Any, Any, GeometricIntegratorsBase.GeometricIntegrator{var\"#s176\", var\"#s175\", CT} where {var\"#s176\"<:SpaceTimeGalerkin, var\"#s175\"<:LagrangianPDEProblem, CT<:GeometricIntegratorsBase.CacheDict{var\"#s175\", var\"#s176\"}}}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"residual!(b, x, sol, params, int)\n\nThe nonlinear residual of one slab step, in the solver's own layout: one equation per free coefficient, ordered as the solver's vector is, and after them the coupling's own equations.\n\nThe equations of the free coefficients are the interior weak form tested against the basis functions that the slab's free rows carry, and the coupling adds its own terms to them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricIntegratorsBase.residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkin}","page":"Library","text":"The ansatz of the method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.assign!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Library","text":"assign!(u, v, w, cache, problem)\n\nThe solution and its two derivatives at the top of the slab, on the output grid, component-major, and beside them the trace (u, v, w) at the nodes of the space quadrature, which trace! fills. Both come from one evaluation of the top of the slab, at two sets of points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.assign!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.bottom_datum-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NoCouplingData}","page":"Library","text":"bottom_datum(data)\n\nThe bottom row of the coefficient array that the coupling fixes, of size (Nx, D), or nothing for a coupling whose free rows are all Nt rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.bottom_datum"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, SpaceTimeAnsatz}","page":"Library","text":"check_ansatz(problem, ansatz)\n\nReject an ansatz that the problem cannot support, when the integrator is built. An ansatz that declares no requirement is accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, SlabCoupling, SpaceTimeAnsatz}","page":"Library","text":"check_coupling(problem, coupling, ansatz)\n\nReject a coupling that the ansatz cannot support, when the integrator is built.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_method-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, SpaceTimeGalerkin}","page":"Library","text":"check_method(problem, method)\n\nReject a method that the problem cannot support, before any step runs.\n\nThe refusals are ArgumentErrors: a target that reads the exact solution of a problem that has none, a fit that needs an ansatz linear in its coefficients on one that is not, a direct linear solve (linear_solver = true) on an ansatz whose step is not affine in its unknowns or a density that is not linear, and what the ansatz's check_ansatz and the coupling's check_coupling refuse.\n\nThe direct linear solve is offered to an ansatz whose step is affine in its unknowns, which has_affine_step reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_method"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_ansatz_data!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Library","text":"copy_ansatz_data!(cache_data, data)\n\nCopy what the ansatz block keeps outside the solver's vector — the parameters it holds fixed — from the cache of the problem's element type into the cache the solver works in, where the solver evaluates at another element type.\n\nAn ansatz whose every unknown is a row of the solver's vector keeps nothing here, and the default does nothing; a network in :last_layer mode frees its output weights alone and leaves its hidden layer in the block, which the block of another element type does not inherit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_ansatz_data!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_coupling_data!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NoCouplingData, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NoCouplingData}","page":"Library","text":"copy_coupling_data!(cache_data, data)\n\nCopy the coupling's datums from the cache of the problem's element type into the cache the solver works in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_coupling_data!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkin}","page":"Library","text":"The coupling of the method, or the coupling that a coupling's block of the cache carries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_data-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{SlabCoupling, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"coupling_data(coupling, ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe coupling's block of the slab cache, built once per integrator. A coupling that keeps no data of its own gets a NoCouplingData.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_data"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_data_type-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{SlabCoupling, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"coupling_data_type(coupling, ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe type of coupling_data(coupling, ansatz, problem, ST), computed without building the block, because the ecosystem asserts the cache's type on every lookup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_data_type"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_guess!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NoCouplingData, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Library","text":"coupling_guess!(data, cache, ansatz, problem, sol)\n\nThe coupling's initial guess of its unknowns, once per slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_guess!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_internal-Tuple{SlabCoupling, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Library","text":"coupling_internal(coupling, ansatz, problem)\n\nThe coupling's own state of a step: the entries of the solution step's internal named tuple, which the ecosystem's copy_internal_variables! fills after every step. A coupling that carries nothing between steps declares no entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_internal"},{"category":"function","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_term!","page":"Library","text":"coupling_term!(b, x, data, cache, ansatz, problem)\n\nThe coupling's own part of the residual, from its block data: its terms on the free rows the interior weak form already carries, and its own equations after them.\n\nThere is no default: a coupling whose residual has no term of its own declares its no-op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_term!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_unknowns-Tuple{SlabCoupling, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Library","text":"coupling_unknowns(coupling, ansatz, problem)\n\nThe number of the coupling's extra unknowns: the entries of the solver's vector that follow the coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_unknowns"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_update!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NoCouplingData, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Library","text":"coupling_update!(data, x, cache, ansatz, problem)\n\nRead the coupling's state out of the solved slab: its unknowns in the solver's vector, and what it hands on to the next slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_update!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.factorisations-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache}","page":"Library","text":"factorisations(cache::SpaceTimeGalerkinCache)\nfactorisations(int::GeometricIntegrator)\n\nThe number of Jacobian factorisations computed by the direct linear solver since the last reset! of the cache, which is one per run whose guess leaves the parameters the step holds fixed unchanged, and one more at every step whose guess has moved them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.factorisations"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_index-NTuple{5, Integer}","page":"Library","text":"The solver index of the free coefficient of free row a, space function j, component d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_index"},{"category":"function","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows","page":"Library","text":"free_rows(coupling, ansatz)\n\nThe number of free time rows of the slab: the rows of the coefficient array that the solver determines, the time index running fastest over the coefficient part of the solver's vector.\n\nEvery coupling declares its own: one that fixes the bottom row of the coefficient array leaves Nt - 1 free rows, and one that imposes the initial condition weakly leaves all Nt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.has_affine_step-Tuple{Any}","page":"Library","text":"has_affine_step(ansatz)\n\nWhether the ansatz's step is affine in its unknowns, which the direct linear solve (linear_solver = true) needs. An ansatz linear in its coefficients has one; a NetworkAnsatz has one in :last_layer mode alone, whose free output weights enter the step affinely while its hidden layer is data, and not in :all mode, where Newton determines every layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.has_affine_step"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.held_parameters-Tuple{Any}","page":"Library","text":"held_parameters(block)\n\nThe parameters the block's step holds fixed: the ones it reads and never frees, on which the step's Jacobian therefore depends. An ansatz whose every unknown is a row of the solver's vector holds none, and the default is the empty range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.held_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.initial_guess-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkin}","page":"Library","text":"The initial guess of the method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.initial_guess"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.nunknowns-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz, SlabCoupling, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Library","text":"nunknowns(ansatz, coupling, problem)\n\nThe number of the ansatz's own unknowns of one slab: the entries of the solver's vector that precede the coupling's.\n\nA tensor B-spline ansatz has one unknown per free row of its coefficient array, per space function and per component, so its count is\n\nnunknowns(ansatz::TensorBSplineAnsatz, coupling, problem) =\n    free_rows(coupling, ansatz) * nspace_functions(ansatz) * components(problem)\n\nwith the coupling that leaves the free rows and the problem that fixes the component count in scope. An ansatz that keeps its unknowns as a parameter vector declares its own count instead; a NetworkAnsatz has one per parameter its mode frees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.nunknowns"},{"category":"function","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_bottom!","page":"Library","text":"slab_bottom!(cache, data, problem, ansatz, sol)\n\nFix the coupling's datum of the slab, in its block data.\n\nA coupling implements this for the two cases it distinguishes: the first slab of a run, where the datum comes from the problem's initial condition, and every later slab, where it is what the previous slab carried out. The cache counts its steps, so cache.steps says which case this is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_bottom!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_residual!-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache, AbstractVector, LagrangianPDEProblem, Integer}","page":"Library","text":"slab_residual!(cache, x, problem, nf)\n\nThe interior weak form of the slab that the solver's vector x describes, in the ansatz's block: the ansatz's field at the nodes of the slab's quadrature, with the coupling's fixed bottom row and its transform of the field, the density's derivatives there, and their weak form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_unknowns-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Library","text":"slab_unknowns(ansatz, coupling, problem)\n\nThe size of one slab's nonlinear system: one equation per unknown of the ansatz, and one per unknown the coupling adds after them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_unknowns"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.solve_stats-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache}","page":"Library","text":"solve_stats(cache::SpaceTimeGalerkinCache)\n\nThe records of the Newton solves of the steps taken, as the named tuple (; iterations, residuals, initial_residuals, converged), one entry per step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.solve_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.step_converged-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Library","text":"step_converged(residual, initial_residual, options)\n\nWhether a step's Newton solve converged: residual ≤ f_abstol + f_reltol · initial_residual, with the tolerances of the solver's options. The relative term counts only for a finite initial_residual, as in SimpleSolvers' own residual test, so the bound is finite and a residual of Inf or NaN never converges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.step_converged"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_trace-Union{Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache{ST}}, Tuple{ST}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"top_trace(cache::SpaceTimeGalerkinCache)\n\nThe trace (u, v, w) at the top of the last slab, at the nodes of the space quadrature, component-major.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_trace"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.total_free_coefficients-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkin, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Library","text":"total_free_coefficients(method, problem)\n\nThe size of one slab's nonlinear system: one equation per unknown of the ansatz, and one per unknown the coupling adds after them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.total_free_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace!-Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Library","text":"trace!(cache, problem)\n\nThe trace (u, v, w) at the top of the slab at the nodes of the space quadrature, component-major, into the cache's three trace matrices, with a row per node of the space rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.transform_field!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, MultisymplecticIntegrators.CouplingData, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"Library","text":"transform_field!(u, v, w, data, block, t̂, x, h, problem)\n\nThe coupling's transform of the field, applied to the ansatz's field u and its derivatives v = ∂ₜu and w = ∂ₓu at the points (t̂[q], x[r]) of the slab, as u[q, r, d], in place. t̂ is the normalised time of the slab, so the physical-time derivative of a term ℓ(t̂, x) is ∂_{t̂}ℓ / h. data is the coupling's block and block the ansatz's, so that a transform that is not a function of the point alone — one that projects the field, for instance — reads the parameters the step is solving for.\n\nThe core applies it wherever it evaluates the field: in the residual, at the nodes of the slab's quadrature, and at the top of the slab, in the trace and in the solution. The default leaves the field as the ansatz gives it; a coupling that lifts the field adds its lift here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.transform_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#SimpleSolvers.cache-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{GeometricIntegratorsBase.GeometricIntegrator{var\"#s176\", var\"#s175\", CT} where {var\"#s176\"<:SpaceTimeGalerkin, var\"#s175\"<:LagrangianPDEProblem, CT<:GeometricIntegratorsBase.CacheDict{var\"#s175\", var\"#s176\"}}, Type{ST}}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"cache(int::GeometricIntegrator, ST)\ncache(int::GeometricIntegrator)\n\nThe slab cache at element type ST, and at the problem's own element type.\n\nThe ecosystem's accessor takes the element type as a value, which the compiler cannot specialise on, so the type assertion of the cache dictionary rebuilds the cache's type at every lookup. These two methods take it as a type parameter, which lets that assertion fold away, so that the lookup adds no allocation to one slab's residual evaluation. The transform of HardConstraintLifting allocates on its own: it calls the problem's boundary function at every time node of the slab, and it builds its scratch arrays for a coefficient ansatz at every call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimpleSolvers.cache"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.AxisTabulation","page":"Library","text":"AxisTabulation{T}\n\nThe assembly tables of one axis of the tensor ansatz: at every point of the composite Gauss rule, the indices of the basis functions that are nonzero there and the values of those functions and of their first derivative, together with the nodes and the weights.\n\nThe tables are dense, q × width: a basis function of degree p is nonzero on p + 1 cells, so the support at one point is width functions wide (p + 1 away from an end, wider for a recombined basis, whose functions may reach further). An entry whose index is zero is padding and carries no term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.AxisTabulation"},{"category":"type","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTabulation","page":"Library","text":"SlabTabulation{T}\n\nThe assembly tables of a tensor B-spline ansatz, from its tensor quadrature: one AxisTabulation per direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTabulation"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.boundary_form!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{ST}, AbstractMatrix{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTabulation}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"boundary_form!(load, values, tabulation)\n\nThe weak form of a boundary trace: the load ∫ values(x) φ_j(x) dx of a trace given at the nodes of the space quadrature, on the functions of the space basis.\n\nIt is the top-row weak form that a CoefficientRestriction carries into the next slab, and the initial momentum of the first slab, which is why both are the same construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.boundary_form!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.density_derivatives!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, Any, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, Any}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"density_derivatives!(∂u, ∂v, ∂w, density, u, v, w, params)\n\n∂L/∂u, ∂L/∂v and ∂L/∂w at every point of the quadrature grid, from the sampled field.\n\nBoth a scalar LagrangianDensity and a density whose components couple — the vector density of VectorWave — are evaluated through point_derivatives, which returns the components of each point as D-tuples without allocating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.density_derivatives!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.interior_weak_form!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTabulation, Any}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"interior_weak_form!(residual, ∂u, ∂v, ∂w, tabulation, h)\n\nThe interior weak Euler–Lagrange form of one slab, from the derivatives of the density at the quadrature points.\n\nFor a test function φ it reads\n\nint_0^1  int_x_min^x_max\nBigl( h  fracpartial Lpartial u  phi\n     + fracpartial Lpartial v  partial_hat t phi\n     + h  fracpartial Lpartial w  partial_x phi Bigr)  dx  dhat t \n\nwhich is the variation of the action \\int \\int L \\, dt \\, dx with the derivatives left on the test function, dt = h \\, d\\hat t. The one factor of h that the v term does not carry is the 1 / h of ∂ₜu = ∂_{t̂}u / h, so the three terms are the three of δS with no factor dropped. The residual is that form for every basis function, in the coefficient layout of the slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.interior_weak_form!"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.nquadrature-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.AxisTabulation}","page":"Library","text":"The number of Gauss points of the axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.nquadrature"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_values!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTabulation, Any}} where ST","page":"Library","text":"slab_values!(u, v, w, coeffs, tabulation, h)\n\nThe field and its two derivatives at every point of the quadrature grid, from the coefficient array coeffs of size (Nt, Nx, D), laid out component-major.\n\nv is the derivative in physical time, ∂ₜu = ∂_{t̂}u / h, and w the derivative in space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_values!"},{"category":"function","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.energy_density","page":"Library","text":"energy_density(density, u, v, w, params = density_params(density))\n\nThe Hamiltonian density of a Lagrangian density, v ∂L/∂v − L.\n\nFor a density L(u, v, w) whose field has velocity v = ∂ₜu and slope w = ∂ₓu, the quantity conserved by the continuous problem is the integral of this density over space. It is read off the density's own API — the partial derivative with respect to v and the value — so it costs no new formula per problem, and it is the right notion for a density that couples the components of a vector field as well, where u, v and w are the vectors of the D components at one point and the product is their inner product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.energy_density"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.energy_error-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, SpaceTimeGalerkin, Any}","page":"Library","text":"energy_error(problem, method, traces)\nenergy_error(problem, ansatz, traces)\n\nThe relative deviation of a run's conserved energy from its value at the first stored step: one value per stored step, the first of which is zero.\n\ntraces is the second value integrate returns. The energy of a step is energy_density integrated over the space quadrature at the nodes the trace is stored on, which is why the rule is reached through the ansatz rather than through the problem: a LagrangianPDEProblem holds the space interval and not the rule on it. The deviation is relative to the first step and not to the initial time, because the trace of a step exists only at the top of the slab it solved, and no trace of t₀ is stored.\n\nA run whose first energy vanishes — an all-zero trace, for one — has no relative scale, and its deviation is the absolute one.\n\nA run that stored no trace raises an ArgumentError: the energy of a step is a function of its trace, and no other value of the run carries the velocity and the slope at the quadrature nodes. A trace whose u, v and w hold different numbers of entries, or whose length is not a whole number of the problem's components, raises one as well rather than being read at the nodes it happens to hold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.energy_error"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.energy_error-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, SymbolicAnsatz, Any}","page":"Library","text":"energy_error(problem, ansatz::SymbolicAnsatz, traces)\n\nThe relative energy deviation of a symbolic run, read from the traces through the trace basis' space rule, which is the rule the trace is stored on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.energy_error"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.solution_data-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Library","text":"solution_data(problem, result) -> (t, x, u)\n\nThe solution of a run as the plain values a figure is drawn from: the step times including the problem's initial time, the output grid, and one matrix per component of the field, of size length(t) × length(x). A run that integrate stopped early ends at its last stored trace.\n\nresult is what integrate returns. A figure needs vectors and not a solution object: a solution is tied to the version of GeometricSolutions that built it, so an archived run could not be drawn without pinning that version, while these three values survive a round trip through a file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.solution_data"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_quadrature_weights-Tuple{SymbolicAnsatz}","page":"Library","text":"space_quadrature_weights(ansatz)\n\nThe weights of the ansatz's space quadrature, which a SymbolicAnsatz takes from the trace basis that owns its rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_quadrature_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"library/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_quadrature_weights-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"Library","text":"space_quadrature_weights(ansatz)\n\nThe weights of the ansatz's space quadrature: the measure the trace (u, v, w) of a step is stored on, and the one that integrates the energy density over x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_quadrature_weights"},{"category":"section","location":"couplings/#Couplings-and-initial-guesses","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The couplings that close a slab's system, and the guess that seeds its solve: a target, the four targets this package ships, and the fits that bring an ansatz to one of them.","title":"Couplings and initial guesses"},{"category":"section","location":"couplings/#The-hard-constraint-lifting","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"HardConstraintLifting is the coupling of the network family that imposes a slab's data on the field itself. The field of the slab is P[N] + ℓ: the transfinite projection of the ansatz's field that vanishes at the two ends of xspan and at the bottom of the slab, plus the transfinite interpolant ℓ of the slab's data — the problem's boundary function at the two ends, and the initial condition's bottom on the first slab and the previous slab's top afterwards. The initial and boundary conditions are therefore met by construction and the interior weak form alone is the residual.\n\nThe slab carries no momentum between steps: the coupling adds no surface term and no load at the bottom of the slab, and coupling_unknowns is zero, so the solver's vector is the ansatz's own. That is this coupling's formulation, kept on purpose; a formulation that carries the incoming momentum at the bottom of each slab is not this one.\n\nA NetworkAnsatz and a TensorBSplineAnsatz are both accepted, and an ansatz of any other kind is refused when the integrator is built. For a network the transform projects the field's derivative with respect to every free parameter together with the field, so the residual is the gradient of the slab's discrete action on the free parameters; for a coefficient ansatz there is no parameter derivative and the transform places the data in the field alone, which needs x_boundary = Dirichlet(): the transform reads the field of the slab's two ends at the time nodes of the residual, so a basis that is free there is refused.\n\nSimpleSplines counts the degree of a basis and not its order, so a trace basis of order 4 is built with degree = 3.","title":"The hard-constraint lifting"},{"category":"section","location":"couplings/#The-fits","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"PINNTraining descends a network's free parameters on its misfit against a sampled target, with a fixed step and no optimiser package: the sample's points are drawn once per fit from a copy of the method's generator, so two fits that share one method object draw the same points, each pass over them takes one step per batch of batch points, and the gradient is the parameter derivative the ansatz's block already carries.","title":"The fits"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.CoefficientRestriction","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"CoefficientRestriction() <: SlabCoupling\n\nThe coupling of two consecutive slabs by restriction: the top trace of the previous slab is restricted to the bottom constraints of the current one.\n\nThe bottom trace of a slab is its bottom row of coefficients, so the restriction is the row itself: the previous slab's top row is this slab's bottom datum, unchanged. The momentum flux goes with it. The v term of the interior weak form leaves one boundary term at the bottom of the slab, ∫ ∂L/∂v · φ dx at t̂ = 0, and the previous slab's top row of the weak form is exactly that load at its top — so the flux is not re-integrated from a trace but read off the row the previous slab already computed. At the first slab both come from the problem's initial condition.\n\nThe coupling adds no other term, because the interior weak form enforces everything else the slab needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.CoefficientRestriction"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.RestrictionData","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"RestrictionData{ST, C} <: CouplingData\n\nThe block of a coupling that restricts the bottom row of the coefficient array: the coupling, the bottom row bottom of size (Nx, D), which is the datum of the step, and the momentum load flux that enters the slab with it, of the same size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.RestrictionData"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_term!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractVector{ST}, Any, MultisymplecticIntegrators.RestrictionData{ST}, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache{ST}, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"coupling_term!(b, x, data::RestrictionData, cache, ansatz, problem)\n\nThe momentum load of the coupling on the row of the bottom test function, ∫ ∂L/∂v · φ₁ ψ_j dx at t̂ = 0, which the previous slab's top weak form already computed. Every other row is the interior weak form's alone, so no other entry changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_term!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.initial_trace!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.RestrictionData{ST}, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache{ST}, LagrangianPDEProblem, TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2})}} where {ST, T}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"initial_trace!(data, cache, problem, ansatz)\n\nThe bottom datum of the first slab — the problem's initial condition, interpolated on the space basis — and the momentum load that goes with it, ∫ ∂L/∂v(u₀, v₀, w₀) φ_j dx at the nodes of the space quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.initial_trace!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.momentum_load!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{ST}, Any, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The momentum ∂L/∂v of a trace at the quadrature nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.momentum_load!"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.BSplineMultiplier","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The multiplier basis of the ansatz's own trace basis, the functions the coupling keeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.BSplineMultiplier"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangeMultiplier","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The multiplier basis in nodal functions at the spline nodes the coupling keeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangeMultiplier"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierBasis","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"Abstract type of the basis that a weak boundary condition's multiplier is expanded in: BSplineMultiplier or LagrangeMultiplier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierBasis"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierCoupling","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"Abstract type of a coupling that imposes the slab's data weakly with surface multipliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierCoupling"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierData","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The block of a weak-multiplier coupling C in the slab cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierData"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions(; multiplier_basis = :bspline) <: SlabCoupling\n\nThe initial condition and the space boundary conditions of a slab, imposed weakly.\n\nThe initial condition is imposed as WeakInitialCondition imposes it. The multipliers at x = a and at x = b are unknowns of the step as well, and their rows are\n\nint bigl( g_a(t) - u(t a) bigr)  chi_p(t)  dt \nqquad\nint bigl( g_b(t) - u(t b) bigr)  chi_p(t)  dt \n\nwhere g_a and g_b are the problem's side traces, evaluated at each node of the time rule, so that inhomogeneous data is imposed as it stands. The multiplier basis is the ansatz's own trace basis, or the nodal basis of that same set of functions, as multiplier_basis (alias basis) names it, so that a slab with Nt time functions and Nx space functions has Nx - 2 multipliers at the bottom surface, without its two corner functions, and Nt at each end of xspan.\n\nThe boundary rows carry an unknown, so the ansatz must take x_boundary = Free(); every other value throws an ArgumentError naming the condition the coupling needs and the one it got. A Dirichlet() basis in particular makes the trace vanish at each end of xspan, which would leave the rows empty.\n\nmethod = SpaceTimeGalerkin(\n    TensorBSplineAnsatz(degree = 2, ncells = (2, 20), xspan = (0, 1),\n        x_boundary = Free()),\n    WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.WeakInitialCondition","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"WeakInitialCondition() <: SlabCoupling\nWeakInitialCondition(; multiplier_basis = :bspline) <: SlabCoupling\n\nThe initial condition of a slab, imposed weakly.\n\nThe bottom row of the coefficient array is free, so all Nt rows of the slab are unknowns, and the slab is closed by one row per multiplier function ψ_p,\n\nint bigl( u_0(x) - u(0 x) bigr)  psi_p(x)  dx \n\nwhere u₀ is the problem's initial condition on the first slab and the previous slab's top trace on every later one. The multiplier at the bottom surface enters the momentum balance as the momentum that enters the slab: on the first slab, the quadrature-weighted L² projection of ∂L/∂v(u₀, v₀, w₀) at the nodes of the space rule onto the multiplier functions, and the previous slab's top multiplier afterwards.\n\nmultiplier_basis names the basis of ψ_p: :bspline, the ansatz's own trace basis, or :lagrange, the nodal basis of that same set of functions. basis is an alias of the keyword.\n\nThe coupling imposes no space boundary condition, so the ends of xspan are left to the space basis: the ansatz must take x_boundary = Dirichlet(), and every other value throws an ArgumentError that names the condition the coupling needs and the one it got. A Free() basis leaves the ends unconstrained, and the remaining conditions constrain them in a way the interior weak form does not carry.\n\nmethod = SpaceTimeGalerkin(\n    TensorBSplineAnsatz(degree = 2, ncells = (2, 20), xspan = (0, 1)),\n    WeakInitialCondition())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.WeakInitialCondition"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierCoupling, NetworkAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"check_coupling(problem, coupling, ansatz)\n\nRefuse a coupling that a network cannot take.\n\nA network holds no bottom coefficient row, so its interior weak form alone is not the gradient of the slab's action: the weak initial-and-boundary coupling adds the four surface terms that complete it, and the refusals of the other couplings name the one that is taken. The trace basis' own x_boundary is not checked, because the network builds that basis with Free(), the condition the weak initial-and-boundary coupling needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierCoupling, SymbolicAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"check_coupling(problem, coupling, ansatz)\n\nRefuse a coupling that a symbolic ansatz cannot take.\n\nA parameter ansatz has no bottom coefficient row, so its interior weak form alone is not the gradient of the slab's action; the weak initial-and-boundary coupling adds the four boundary terms that complete it. The refusals of the other couplings name the one that is taken.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierCoupling, NetworkAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The free rows of a network slab are its free parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.MultiplierCoupling, SymbolicAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The free rows of a symbolic slab are its parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLifting","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"HardConstraintLifting() <: SlabCoupling\n\nThe coupling of the hard-constraint formulation: the field of the slab is the ansatz's field projected onto the slab's data, plus the data.\n\nThe projection is the transfinite one, which vanishes at the two ends of xspan and at the bottom of the slab,\n\nPN = N - T_1N + T_2Nquad\nT_1N = psi_L(x) N(hat t a) + psi_R(x) N(hat t b) + (1 - hat t) N(0 x)quad\nT_2N = (1 - hat t) left(psi_L(x) N(0 a) + psi_R(x) N(0 b)right)\n\nwith ψ_L(x) = (b - x)/(b - a) and ψ_R(x) = (x - a)/(b - a), and the lift is its counterpart on the slab's data,\n\nell = psi_L g_a(t) + psi_R g_b(t) + (1 - hat t) u_b(x)\n       - (1 - hat t) left(psi_L g_a(t_0) + psi_R g_b(t_0)right)\n\nat the physical time t = t_0 + h \\hat t, with g_a and g_b the problem's bcs_function at the two ends and u_b the bottom datum. The field of the slab is P[N] + ℓ, so it is the data at the three parts of the boundary by construction: P[N] is zero there and ℓ is the datum.\n\nThe corner correction is the term ψ_L(x) * (1 - t̂) at the left end and ψ_R(x) * (1 - t̂) at the right. With it ℓ interpolates the sides at the slab's start and the bottom along the whole slab; without it the bottom would carry the sides' values at t_0 as well, and only the second slab, whose sides at t_0 are its predecessor's top, would show it.\n\nThe coupling carries no unknown of its own — no multiplier and no coefficient of its own — so coupling_unknowns is zero, coupling_term! adds nothing to the residual, and the slab hands the next one its top row alone. The interior weak form is the whole residual: there is no surface term and no load at the bottom of the slab.\n\nThe side values are the problem's boundary function and the bottom is its initial condition on the first slab and the previous slab's top afterwards, so the coupling reads no exact solution. A boundary function that does not meet the initial condition at a corner is a warning, once per integrator, and never an error.\n\nA NetworkAnsatz and a TensorBSplineAnsatz are both accepted, and an ansatz of any other kind is refused when the integrator is built; the coefficient ansatz takes x_boundary = Dirichlet(). For a network the transform projects the field's derivative with respect to every free parameter together with the field, because the projection is linear in the parameters. A coefficient ansatz carries no parameter derivative, and its rows are its own basis functions rather than the projected ones: there the transform places the data in the field and the residual is a record of what the placement leaves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLifting"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"HardConstraintLiftingData{ST, C} <: CouplingData\n\nThe block of the lifting: the coupling, the slab's start time start, the datum's u and its space derivative w at the nodes of the rule the residual reads, and one scratch vector work.\n\nstart is the physical time of the slab's bottom, so that a side value is read at start + h t̂, and it moves with the slab; the datum's two arrays move with it too.\n\nwork is laid out in four parts, the last empty where the ansatz carries no basis of its own:\n\nthe field of the two corners, (0, a) and (0, b), as [u; ∂_t̂u; ∂ₓu], 6D entries;\ntheir derivative with respect to the nf free parameters, 6D · nf entries, entry (i, k) of the k-th parameter at 6D + (k - 1) · 6D + i;\nthe space nodes of the datum, one per row of bottom_u;\nthe traces of the basis of a coefficient ansatz at the three boundary parts: the space functions at the two ends of xspan, 2 · Nx entries, and the time functions at the slab's bottom, Nt entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, HardConstraintLifting, NetworkAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"check_coupling(problem, ::HardConstraintLifting, ansatz)\n\nRefuse an ansatz the lifting cannot take, and a coefficient basis whose ends are not the data's.\n\nThe lifting's transform places the data in the field of a network slab and of a coefficient slab; a SymbolicAnsatz carries neither, so it is refused. A coefficient slab needs x_boundary = Dirichlet(): the transform reads the field of the slab's two ends at the time nodes of the residual, so a Free() space basis — free at the ends, and lifted there — would be lifted at the wrong time.\n\nThe corner check of the slab's data is not a refusal and is not here: the block's coupling_data reports it, once per integrator, as a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_coupling"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_coupling_data!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"copy_coupling_data!(cache_data, data::HardConstraintLiftingData)\n\nCopy the datum from the cache of the problem's element type into the cache the solver evaluates in, which the residual does before every evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_coupling_data!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_data-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{HardConstraintLifting, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem{T}, Type{ST}}} where {T, ST}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"coupling_data(c::HardConstraintLifting, ansatz, problem::LagrangianPDEProblem{T}, ST)\n\nThe lifting's block, built once for each cache that an integrator builds.\n\nThe cache of the problem's own element type T also reports a corner at which the problem's boundary function and its initial condition disagree, and the cache that a solver builds for another element type — a Dual on its first Jacobian — is built on top of that one and says nothing, so one integrator warns once.\n\nThe projection fixes the slab at the two ends in x and at its bottom, so the corners (0, a) and (0, b) carry two data. A problem whose boundary function is its exact solution meets its initial condition there exactly; a problem whose data disagree still runs, with the difference carried by the corner correction, and the warning names the corner and the difference so that such a run is visible in its log.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_data"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_term!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"coupling_term!(b, x, data::HardConstraintLiftingData, cache, ansatz, problem)\n\nThe lifting's own part of the residual, which is none: the field carries the data by construction, so the interior weak form alone is the slab's residual and no surface row is added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_term!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_unknowns-Tuple{HardConstraintLifting, SpaceTimeAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"coupling_unknowns(::HardConstraintLifting, ansatz, problem)\n\nThe lifting's own unknowns, which are none: the data are imposed by construction and no multiplier is carried, so the solver's vector is the ansatz's own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.coupling_unknowns"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows-Tuple{HardConstraintLifting, NetworkAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The free rows of a network slab are its free parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows-Tuple{HardConstraintLifting, SpaceTimeAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"free_rows(::HardConstraintLifting, ansatz::SpaceTimeAnsatz)\n\nThe free rows of a coefficient slab are all its time functions: the coupling fixes no row of the coefficient array, and the slab's bottom is imposed on the field instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.lifting_datum-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractVector{ST}, AbstractVector, Any, Any, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"lifting_datum(column, nodes, x, left, right)\n\nThe datum of one component at x, where left and right are its values at the two ends of xspan.\n\nThe datum is stored at nodes, the space nodes of the ansatz's own rule, where this returns the node's own value: the residual and the trace read the datum exactly. Between two nodes the value is their linear blend, and beyond them — the top of the slab is evaluated on the output grid, whose points include the two ends — it is left or right, the datum's value there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.lifting_datum"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_bottom!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData{ST}, LagrangianPDEProblem, SpaceTimeAnsatz, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"slab_bottom!(cache, data::HardConstraintLiftingData, problem, ansatz, sol)\n\nFix the lifting's datum: the slab's start time, and the bottom's u and w at the space nodes of the ansatz's rule. The first slab takes them from the problem's initial condition, and every later slab from the top of the slab before it, which is where the lifting left the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_bottom!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.transform_field!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, Any, Any, Any, LagrangianPDEProblem}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, Any, Any, Any, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, Any, Any, Any, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any, Any}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, Any, Any, Any, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any, Any, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"transform_field!(u, v, w, data, block::NetworkSlab, t̂, x, h, problem, Ju, Jv, Jw)\n\nThe lifting's transform of a network's field: the field, its two derivatives and — where Ju, Jv and Jw are this evaluation's — its derivative with respect to every free parameter become P[N] + ℓ at the nodes (t̂[q], x[r]).\n\nThe three parameter-derivative arrays default to the block's own, which are the ones the residual's evaluation filled. The core also transforms the top of the slab, at one time node and at another set of space points; that evaluation writes no parameter derivative, and the block's arrays are left alone there because their sizes are the residual's and not the top's.\n\nThe transform reads the ansatz's block and never the ansatz object, so it projects the parameters the step is solving for: block.values, block.gradient and the block's surfaces are one evaluation of block.params. It writes the field arrays alone, so the second call of a step — the one in update! — compounds nothing of the slab before it into the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.transform_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.transform_field!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.HardConstraintLiftingData{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab{ST}, Any, Any, Any, LagrangianPDEProblem}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"transform_field!(u, v, w, data, block::TensorBSplineSlab, t̂, x, h, problem)\n\nThe lifting's transform of a coefficient slab's field: P[N] + ℓ at the nodes (t̂[q], x[r]), for the field and its two derivatives.\n\nA coefficient ansatz carries no parameter derivative, and its rows are its own basis functions rather than the projected ones; the transform therefore places the data in the field and the residual of such a slab records what the placement leaves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.transform_field!"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.OrthogonalGreedy","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"OrthogonalGreedy(; bias_interval = (-π, π), Nw = 50, Nb = 50) <: SlabFitMethod\n\nThe fit method of an ansatz whose parameters enter a nonlinearity, such as a NetworkAnsatz: a dictionary of σ(w₁ t̂ + w₂ x + b) atoms, from which the fit selects one atom per hidden unit and reads the output weights by least squares.\n\nThe dictionary is Nw + 1 directions of equal angle on the half-open circle [0, 2π), crossed with Nb + 1 biases over bias_interval. The fit is linear in the output weights alone, so the hidden weights and the biases stay where the dictionary put them; a Projection() is refused on such an ansatz, and this fit replaces it as the ansatz's default_fit.\n\nmethod = SpaceTimeGalerkin(NetworkAnsatz(S = 10, xspan = (0.0, 1.0), ncells = (2, 5),\n        degree = 3), WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions();\n    initial_guess = FitTo(ExactSolution(); method = OrthogonalGreedy(Nw = 50, Nb = 50)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.OrthogonalGreedy"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.PINNTraining","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"PINNTraining(; epochs = 100, step = 1e-2, batch = 100, nsamples = 5_000,\n             rng = Xoshiro(1234)) <: SlabFitMethod\n\nThe fit method of a NetworkAnsatz that trains every free parameter of the network against a sample of the slab's target, by plain gradient descent: epochs passes over the nsamples points of the slab's target, each pass cut into consecutive batches of batch points in their drawn order, with one step of the fixed step per batch on that batch's mean squared residual.\n\nThe points are drawn once per fit, uniformly over [0, 1] × xspan, from a copy of rng taken at the call and never from the process's global generator, so one method object gives every integrator that shares it the same points and the same fitted parameters. The target is evaluated at each of them by target_point!, so every target of the guess is read at the physical time of its point. The gradient is the derivative of the field with respect to each free parameter, which the ansatz's block already carries: the fit needs no differentiation and no optimiser package.\n\nThe descent writes the free parameters alone and leaves the parameters the mode holds fixed at their values, so a :last_layer network keeps its hidden layer. A fresh block's hidden layer is zero and no PINNTraining fit writes it, so on a :last_layer network of tanh units the field and its derivative with respect to every output weight stay zero, and the fit leaves the field at zero. Its one buffer, the batch gradient, is allocated once per call, before the epoch loop, and no epoch and no batch allocates.\n\nmethod = SpaceTimeGalerkin(NetworkAnsatz(S = 10, xspan = (0.0, 1.0), ncells = (2, 5),\n        degree = 3), WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions();\n    initial_guess = FitTo(InitialCondition(); method = PINNTraining(epochs = 100)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.PINNTraining"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Projection","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"Projection() <: SlabFitMethod\n\nThe fit method of an ansatz that is linear in its coefficients: the L² projection of the target onto the slab's ansatz space, on the quadrature of the slab.\n\nThe projection solves the mass-matrix system of the ansatz, which SimpleSplines factorises once per quadrature rather than once per step. An ansatz whose coefficients enter a nonlinearity has no such system, and a Projection() on one is refused at construction — see SpaceTimeGalerkin.\n\nA user-defined fit method is a subtype of SlabFitMethod, and places the coefficients of coeffs, of size (Nt, Nx, D) for a tensor ansatz and the parameter vector of a network, from the sampled target, in one method of\n\nfit!(coeffs, method, ansatz, sample, cache)\n\ncache is the SpaceTimeGalerkinCache of the slab, which a fit may read; a projection keeps no scratch in it, and a network's block holds the workspace of its greedy fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Projection"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.TrainedHiddenLayer","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"TrainedHiddenLayer(; epochs = 100, learning_rate = 1e-5, rng = Xoshiro(1234)) <: SlabFitMethod\n\nThe fit method of a NetworkAnsatz in :last_layer mode that trains the hidden layer instead of taking it from a dictionary: every epoch takes one gradient-descent step on the hidden weights and the hidden biases, and then reads the output weights by least squares on the hidden features that step left.\n\nThe misfit the step descends is the field's distance from the sample of the slab, weighted as the slab's own measure weights it on the normalised time: the ansatz's interior rule by ωₜ ωₓ, the two surfaces at the space rule by ωₓ, and the two surfaces at the time rule by ωₜ. The features the solve reads are the hidden layer's activations at those same points, so the four surfaces carry the initial and boundary data into the fit.\n\nepochs = 0 fits the output layer alone on the hidden layer the rng draws; a larger epochs descends first. The draw is taken from rng and never from the process's global generator, and the generator is copied per call, so one method object gives the same fit to every integrator that shares it. The buffers of the fit live in the ansatz's block, not in the method.\n\nA network in :all mode is refused, because Newton determines the hidden layer there.\n\nmethod = SpaceTimeGalerkin(NetworkAnsatz(S = 20, mode = :last_layer, xspan = (0.0, 1.0),\n        ncells = (2, 5), degree = 3), WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions();\n    initial_guess = FitTo(InitialCondition(); method = TrainedHiddenLayer(epochs = 20)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.TrainedHiddenLayer"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.default_fit-Tuple{SpaceTimeAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"default_fit(ansatz)\n\nThe fit method FitTo(target) uses when the caller names none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.default_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!-Tuple{Any, PINNTraining, SpaceTimeAnsatz, Any, Any}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"fit!(coeffs, method::PINNTraining, ansatz, sample, cache)\n\nRefuse a PINNTraining fit on an ansatz that is not a NetworkAnsatz: the descent reads the derivative of the field with respect to each free parameter, and only a network's block carries it. The two apply_guess! methods that fit — a coefficient ansatz's and a network's — call fit!, so the refusal is this one method, at the first fit rather than when the integrator is built. An ansatz whose apply_guess! has no FitTo method — a SymbolicAnsatz, for one — raises a MethodError there, before any fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.is_linear_in_coefficients-Tuple{SpaceTimeAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"is_linear_in_coefficients(ansatz)\n\nWhether the ansatz is linear in its coefficients. A Projection() needs one that is; an ansatz whose coefficients enter a nonlinearity reports false and is refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.is_linear_in_coefficients"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Continuation","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"Continuation() <: SlabInitialGuess\n\nThe default initial guess: the previous slab's solution, carried into the new slab.\n\nFor the tensor B-spline ansatz under a CoefficientRestriction that is the previous slab's top coefficient row, repeated in every row: each row of the new slab starts where the previous slab ended. At the first slab there is nothing to carry, and the guess falls back to FitTo(InitialCondition()) with the ansatz's default fit method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Continuation"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ExactSolution","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"ExactSolution() <: SlabTarget\n\nThe problem's exact solution u(t, x) at the physical time of the slab.\n\nA problem without an exact solution cannot supply it, and FitTo(ExactSolution()) on one throws an ArgumentError at construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ExactSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.FitTo","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"FitTo(target; method = nothing) <: SlabInitialGuess\n\nFit the ansatz to target on the slab, with the fit method method.\n\nA target is evaluated on the slab — see InitialCondition, PreviousSlab, ExactSolution and TargetFunction — and the fit brings the ansatz to it. A method that is named is used as it is; an unnamed one resolves through default_fit(ansatz), so that an ansatz with a fit method of its own gets it.\n\ntarget is a SlabTarget and method a SlabFitMethod; a value of another role, such as FitTo(Continuation()), has no method and is refused at construction.\n\nA user-defined target is a subtype of SlabTarget with one method of\n\nevaluate!(sample, target, cache, ansatz, problem, sol)\n\nfilling sample, of size (Qt, Qx, D), with the target's value at the quadrature points of the slab. The guess allocates sample and the fitted coefficients once per step; the slab cache holds no scratch of the guess.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.FitTo"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.InitialCondition","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"InitialCondition() <: SlabTarget\n\nThe problem's initial condition u₀(x), constant in time over the slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.InitialCondition"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.PreviousSlab","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"PreviousSlab() <: SlabTarget\n\nThe previous slab's solution, extrapolated in time beyond the top of that slab.\n\nFor the B-spline ansatz the extrapolation is the polynomial piece of the slab's top time cell, expanded about its top and evaluated at the displacement t̂ beyond it: a spline is a polynomial on each cell, so that piece extends the solution without inventing anything. At the first slab, where there is no previous solution, the target falls back to InitialCondition().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.PreviousSlab"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.TargetFunction","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"TargetFunction(f) <: SlabTarget\n\nA user-defined target: f(t, x, params) in physical time.\n\nThe result is converted to the problem's element type, so a Float32 problem started from a function that returns Float64 keeps Float32 coefficients. A D-component problem may return a number, which every component takes, or a vector of the D values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.TargetFunction"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#GeometricIntegratorsBase.initial_guess!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, GeometricIntegratorsBase.GeometricIntegrator{var\"#s210\", var\"#s209\", CT} where {var\"#s210\"<:SpaceTimeGalerkin, var\"#s209\"<:LagrangianPDEProblem, CT<:GeometricIntegratorsBase.CacheDict{var\"#s209\", var\"#s210\"}}}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"initial_guess!(sol, history, params, int)\n\nFill the nonlinear solver's vector of the slab from the method's initial guess, and then the coupling's own unknowns from the guess the coupling defines for them.\n\nAn ansatz whose unknowns are not coefficients supplies its own hooks: a network's are apply_guess!(c, guess::Continuation, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, …), which carries its previous parameters, and apply_guess!(c, guess::FitTo, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, …), which fits its target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricIntegratorsBase.initial_guess!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, InitialCondition, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache, TensorBSplineAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"evaluate!(sample, target, cache, ansatz, problem, sol)\n\nFill sample, of size (Qt, Qx, D), with the value of target at the quadrature points of the slab, of which sol fixes the physical time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_method-Tuple{FitTo, SpaceTimeAnsatz}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"fit_method(fit::FitTo, ansatz)\n\nThe fit method a FitTo uses: the one it names, or the ansatz's own default_fit where the caller named none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_method"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.needs_exact_solution-Tuple{SlabTarget}","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"Whether a target needs the problem's exact solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.needs_exact_solution"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.target_values!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, Any, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTabulation, TensorBSplineAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"target_values!(sample, f, tabulation, ansatz, problem, sol)\n\nThe value of a pointwise target f(t, x, params) on the slab, per component, converted to the problem's element type. A f that returns a number gives every component that number; one that returns a vector gives component d its d-th entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.target_values!"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkFit","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"The workspace of one fit: its dictionary, the table of that dictionary at the slab's nodes, the buffers of the selection, and the method whose parameters built them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkFit"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_network!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkFit{ST}, OrthogonalGreedy, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SlabTabulation, Integer, Integer, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"Fit S atoms of D components to sample by the orthogonal greedy algorithm of OrthogonalGreedy, in place throughout, and return the number of atoms taken: the dictionary and its table are built only when the sizes or the method's parameters change, a fit after that allocates nothing, and an atom shorter than √eps adds no rank. coefficients leaves the triangular solve of the fit's Gram matrix, that is the output weights of the atoms taken.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_network!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.greedy_atoms-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{OrthogonalGreedy, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"greedy_atoms(method::OrthogonalGreedy, ::Type{T})\n\nThe dictionary of the fit, as (w₁, w₂, b) rows of precision T: Nw + 1 directions of equal angle on the half-open circle [0, 2π), crossed with Nb + 1 biases over bias_interval. The grid is half-open, so no direction is repeated and every direction of the circle appears once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.greedy_atoms"},{"category":"type","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.TrainedFit","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"TrainedFit{ST}\n\nThe workspace of a TrainedHiddenLayer fit in the block of a network ansatz: the sample the fit is taken on and the buffers of its two steps, which a fit of other sizes builds again.\n\npoints holds the normalised time and the space coordinate of every point of the sample, measure the weight the slab's measure gives that point, and target and values the target and the field there, one row per point and one column per component. features holds the hidden layer's activations at the points, weighted by the square root of measure, and rhs the target weighted the same way; features \\ rhs is the least-squares problem whose solution is the output layer. gradient is the misfit's derivative with respect to the hidden parameters, and misfit the misfit of the fitted field.\n\nThe buffers are of the problem's element type. points and measure depend on the block's rules alone, and every fit overwrites the rest, so the method holds no state of the fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.TrainedFit"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_layout!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TrainedFit{ST}, Any, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"fit_layout!(f, ansatz, s)\n\nBuild the sample the fit is taken on: the points of the ansatz's own interior rule, then the four surfaces at the nodes of the trace basis' rules, and the weight the slab's measure gives each of them — ωₜ ωₓ in the interior, ωₓ at the two surfaces in time and ωₜ at the two in space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_layout!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_trained!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TrainedFit{ST}, TrainedHiddenLayer, AbstractVector{ST}, Any, NamedTuple, Any}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"fit_trained!(f, method, w, ansatz, sample, s)\n\nFit the hidden layer of a :last_layer network and its output layer to the sample: draw the hidden layer from the method's generator, read the output weights of that draw by least squares, and then, once per epoch, take one gradient-descent step on the hidden parameters and read the output weights again. epochs = 0 leaves the drawn hidden layer in place and fits the output layer alone.\n\nsample is the fit's sample (; interior, faces) of fit_sample. The draw is taken once in Float64 and rounded to the element type, so that one generator gives one hidden layer in every precision, up to rounding.\n\nThe parameters are written into w in the order L1.W, L1.b, L2.W; w's hidden part — the entries before first_free, which the mode does not free — is the draw's, whatever it held before.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_trained!"},{"category":"method","location":"couplings/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.misfit-Union{Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TrainedFit{ST}}, Tuple{ST}} where ST","page":"Couplings and initial guesses","text":"misfit(f::TrainedFit)\n\nThe weighted misfit of the field at the sample: Σ_k ω_k Σ_d (u_kd − f_kd)².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.misfit"},{"category":"section","location":"seam/#The-slab-seam","page":"The slab seam","text":"A SpaceTimeGalerkin method holds three parts: an ansatz, a coupling and an initial guess. The core of the integrator — residual!, update! and integrate_step! — knows none of them by type. It reaches each part through the functions on this page, and each part keeps its own data in a block of the slab cache.","title":"The slab seam"},{"category":"section","location":"seam/#The-cache","page":"The slab seam","text":"The SpaceTimeGalerkinCache holds what every method fills, whatever its parts:\n\nfield what it holds filled by\nx the solver's vector of the slab the guess, then the Newton solver\nu, v, w the field and its derivatives at the points of the slab's quadrature the ansatz, then the coupling's transform\ndu, dv, dw the density's derivatives at those points the core\ntrace_u, trace_v, trace_w the field at the top of the last slab, at the nodes of the space quadrature the ansatz, then the coupling's transform\niterations, residuals, initial_residuals, converged the record of every step's Newton solve the core\nsteps the number of steps taken in the run the core\nansatz the ansatz's block the ansatz\ncoupling the coupling's block the coupling\n\nThe guess keeps nothing in the cache: it allocates its scratch once per step.","title":"The cache"},{"category":"section","location":"seam/#The-ansatz","page":"The slab seam","text":"The ansatz discretises the field on one slab, and owns the quadrature of the slab. Its block is built by ansatz_data, whose type ansatz_data_type gives without building it. For a TensorBSplineAnsatz the block is a TensorBSplineSlab: the coefficient array, its weak form, the top row of the last slab, the tables of the slab's quadrature, and the space basis at the output grid and at the trace nodes. For a SymbolicAnsatz the block is a SymbolicSlab: the parameter vector, its weak form, the field and its parameter Jacobians at the nodes and on the four edges of the slab, the tables of the slab's quadrature, and the trace basis' space basis at the output grid and at the trace nodes. For a NetworkAnsatz the block is a NetworkSlab: the parameter vector, its weak form, the field and its parameter derivative at the nodes of the ansatz's own rule and on the four edges of the slab, the tables of that rule and of the trace basis', and the scratch of one point evaluation.\n\nThe core calls these functions of the ansatz, each on its block:\n\nquadrature_points: the time and the space nodes of the slab's quadrature;\nslab_field!: the field and its derivatives at those nodes, from the solver's vector and the coupling's fixed bottom row;\nslab_weak_form! and free_residual!: the weak form of the density's derivatives, and its rows of the free coefficients in the solver's layout;\nkeep_top!: keep the top row of the solved slab, which a symbolic ansatz, with no rows, does not have;\ntop_values!, with output_evaluation, trace_evaluation and evaluation_points: the field at the top of the slab, at the output grid and at the trace nodes, from one evaluation;\ncheck_ansatz: the refusal of an ansatz that the problem cannot support;\ncopy_ansatz_data!: what the block keeps outside the solver's vector, from the cache of the problem's element type into the cache the solver works in; the default copies nothing.\n\nThe solver's vector of a slab holds the ansatz's unknowns and then the coupling's. nunknowns counts the ansatz's: for a tensor B-spline ansatz the free rows of its ntime_functions, times nspace_functions, times the components, and for a network the parameters its mode frees. slab_unknowns adds the coupling's unknowns. A symbolic ansatz counts its parameters, and total_free_coefficients gives the size for every ansatz.","title":"The ansatz"},{"category":"section","location":"seam/#The-coupling","page":"The slab seam","text":"The coupling joins consecutive slabs: it fixes the datum of a slab, lays out the free rows of the solver's vector, adds its terms and its own equations to the residual, and may transform the field. Its block is a CouplingData that carries the coupling itself, built by coupling_data, with its type from coupling_data_type. A coupling that keeps nothing gets a NoCouplingData; CoefficientRestriction keeps a RestrictionData, its bottom row and its momentum load; the weak couplings keep a MultiplierData, their multipliers and the tables of their trace basis.\n\nBefore a block exists, the core asks the coupling itself:\n\nfree_rows: the free time rows, declared by every coupling, with no default;\ncoupling_unknowns: the number of the coupling's own unknowns;\ncheck_coupling: the refusal of an ansatz that the coupling cannot support;\ncoupling_internal: the coupling's entries of the solution step's internal state.\n\nWithin a step, every function of the coupling dispatches on its block:\n\nslab_bottom!: fix the datum of the slab;\nbottom_datum: the bottom row the coupling fixes, or nothing;\ntransform_field!: the coupling's transform of the field, wherever the core evaluates the field — in the residual, the trace and the solution; the default leaves the field as it is;\ncoupling_term!: the coupling's terms of the residual, with no default, so that a coupling without a term declares its no-op;\ncoupling_guess!: the guess of the coupling's own unknowns;\ncoupling_update!: what the coupling reads out of the solved slab and hands on;\ncopy_coupling_data!: the datum, from the cache of the problem's element type into the cache the solver works in.","title":"The coupling"},{"category":"section","location":"seam/#The-guess","page":"The slab seam","text":"The initial guess fills the solver's vector before each solve, through initial_guess!: Continuation repeats the previous slab's top row, or, for a symbolic ansatz, takes the ansatz's start vector on the first slab and the previous slab's parameters after it, and FitTo evaluates a SlabTarget on the slab with evaluate! and fits the ansatz to it with a SlabFitMethod through fit!, whose contract Projection states. The coupling then guesses its own unknowns.","title":"The guess"},{"category":"section","location":"seam/#A-step","page":"The slab seam","text":"integrate_step! runs one slab: slab_bottom!, the guess, the Newton solve, the record of the solve, and update!, which evaluates the solved slab, writes the solution and the trace, and calls coupling_update!. integrate drives the steps and returns the solution, the traces and the solve_stats of the run.","title":"A step"},{"category":"section","location":"seam/#The-action-form-(U17)","page":"The slab seam","text":"The interior weak form of a slab is the gradient of its discrete action with respect to the parameters ĉ of the ansatz, the whole coefficient array for the tensor B-spline ansatz and the parameter vector for the symbolic ansatz, and the residual keeps the rows of it that the free rows carry. The action is the sum over the nodes of the slab's quadrature of the weighted density L(u, v, w), where u, v and w are the field and its derivatives that ĉ gives at the nodes. By the chain rule, the gradient is the pullback of the density's derivatives ∂u, ∂v, ∂w at the nodes through the map from ĉ to the field there. The core already evaluates the residual in that order, one seam function per factor:\n\nslab_field! applies the field map: it builds ĉ from the solver's vector and the coupling's bottom row, and gives the field at the nodes;\ntransform_field! applies the coupling's transform; a lift ℓ(t̂, x) that does not depend on ĉ changes the point at which the density is evaluated and not the pullback;\nthe core evaluates ∂u, ∂v and ∂w of the density at the nodes;\nslab_weak_form! pulls them back through the field map, with the quadrature weights;\nfree_residual! writes the rows of the free coefficients in the solver's layout.\n\nFor the tensor B-spline ansatz the field map is linear in ĉ, so its pullback is the product with the tabulated basis, which is the weak form in src/core/weak_form.jl. An ansatz whose field map is not linear, such as a network, implements slab_field! as its map and slab_weak_form! as the pullback of ∂u, ∂v and ∂w through that map by automatic differentiation, with free_residual! on its own block. The symbolic ansatz is such an ansatz, and its pullback reads the parameter Jacobians that it differentiates symbolically when it is built. A coupling may compute coupling_term! as the gradient, by automatic differentiation, of its own action terms: the function receives the solver's vector x and the coupling's block, which is all those terms read. Neither the core nor the existing couplings change for either.\n\nThe variant that the seam carries is the pullback of ∂L at the nodes: the core evaluates the density's derivatives, and the ansatz differentiates only its field map. The seam does not differentiate L inside the ansatz's block, so an ansatz never sees the density, and the density keeps the derivatives that its problem defines.","title":"The action form (U17)"},{"category":"section","location":"ansatz/#The-ansatz-families","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The space-time ansätze: the tensor B-spline basis, the network and the symbolic field. Each keeps its own block in the slab cache and supplies the seam functions that the page The slab seam describes.","title":"The ansatz families"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkAnsatz","page":"The ansatz families","text":"NetworkAnsatz(; S, activation = tanh, mode = :all, xspan, ncells, degree,\n                quadrature_order = nothing, D = 1)\n\nA neural-network ansatz of one space-time slab: a two-layer network whose input is the normalised time t̂ and the physical space coordinate x, and whose output is the D components of the field. NetworkAnsatz <: SpaceTimeAnsatz, so a SpaceTimeGalerkin holds it as its ansatz.\n\nThe network is\n\nu_d(hat t x) = sum_s=1^S W^(2)_ds  sigmabigl(W^(1)_s1 hat t\n                 + W^(1)_s2 x + b_sbigr)\n\nthat is Chain(Dense(2, S, activation), Dense(S, D, identity; use_bias = false)). Its parameters are the hidden weights (S, 2), the hidden biases (S,) and the output weights (D, S), in the order L1.W, L1.b, L2.W of NeuralNetworkParameters, and one hidden layer serves all D components.\n\nmode fixes which parameters the solver determines: :all frees every one of them, and :last_layer frees the output weights alone and leaves the hidden layer where the initial guess put it, so that the step is affine in its unknowns, and two Newton iterations reach its tolerance wherever the output weights carry every constraint row of the coupling. The parameters live in the ansatz's block in the slab cache and never in the ansatz, so two integrators that share one ansatz object give identical results.\n\nThe ansatz carries a TensorBSplineAnsatz as its trace basis, trace_basis(ansatz), built from degree and ncells on the same xspan with Free() at both ends, the condition the weak initial-and-boundary coupling needs. The functions of that basis are the multipliers of a coupling that imposes the slab's data weakly, and its rule is the one the coupling's tables and its surface rows are built on. The field itself is evaluated on a rule of the ansatz's own: quadrature_order Gauss nodes per cell in the time direction, and the space grid of the trace basis, which the cache's trace is kept on. nothing takes one node per cell more than the trace basis has, so that the two rules are not the same one.\n\nAn xspan that does not hold two values, an S or a D that is not positive, and a mode that is neither :all nor :last_layer raise ArgumentError. An activation that the symbolic derivatives of SymbolicNeuralNetworks cannot reach raises that package's own error, which is not caught here.\n\nansatz = NetworkAnsatz(S = 10, xspan = (0.0, 1.0), ncells = (2, 5), degree = 3)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkAnsatz"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkPoints","page":"The ansatz families","text":"NetworkPoints{T}\n\nA set of space points at which a network block evaluates its field: the two surfaces of the slab at the nodes of the space rule, and the output grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkPoints"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab","page":"The ansatz families","text":"NetworkSlab{ST, T, A}\n\nThe block of a NetworkAnsatz in the slab cache: what the ansatz keeps of one slab.\n\nparams is the network's parameter vector of the slab, in the order L1.W, L1.b, L2.W, and residual its interior weak form, one row per free parameter. u, v and w hold the field and its derivatives at the nodes of the ansatz's own rule, and Ju, Jv and Jw their derivative with respect to each free parameter there. bottom, top, left and right hold the field and its parameter derivative at the four surfaces of the slab, on the trace basis' rules, which is where the coupling's rows read them. tabulation is the ansatz's own rule and trace_tabulation the trace basis', which the coupling's tables are built on; output and trace are the two point sets the core evaluates the top of the slab at, and fit the workspace of the greedy fit.\n\nvalues and gradient are the scratch of one evaluation — the 3D values of the field, its two derivatives and their parameter derivative — input its two-entry argument, and parameter_set the parameters laid out as the network's kernels read them, which every evaluation refreshes from params. train is the workspace of the TrainedHiddenLayer fit, which trains the hidden layer in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#GeometricIntegratorsBase.initsolver-Tuple{SimpleSolvers.Newton, SpaceTimeGalerkin{<:NetworkAnsatz}, GeometricIntegratorsBase.CacheDict}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"initsolver(solvermethod, method, caches)\n\nThe network family solves its slab with a rank-revealing linear solve.\n\nA network's rows are its parameters, and the constraint rows of a multiplier coupling depend on the field alone, so the Jacobian is a saddle point whose rank is at most the number of parameters plus the number of constraints, and the constraint block reaches only the output weights in :last_layer mode. The minimum-norm step of a complete orthogonal factorization solves a consistent system exactly and drops the directions the rank does not carry, where a factorisation without rank detection loses the descent direction and raises on the zero pivot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricIntegratorsBase.initsolver"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.activation_function-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The activation of the hidden layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.activation_function"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{NetworkAnsatz{T, C, FT, JT, TB} where {C, FT, JT, TB<:(TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2}))}, LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where {T, ST}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"ansatz_data(ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe ansatz's block of the slab cache, built once per integrator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data_type-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{NetworkAnsatz{T, C, FT, JT, TB} where {C, FT, JT, TB<:(TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2}))}, LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where {T, ST}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"ansatz_data_type(ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe type of ansatz_data(ansatz, problem, ST), computed without building the block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data_type"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.apply_guess!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkinCache{ST}, Continuation, NetworkAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"apply_guess!(cache, guess, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, problem, sol)\n\nFill the solver's vector of a network slab from the method's initial guess.\n\nA continuation carries the previous slab's parameters into the new one, which the block still holds where the previous step left them; at the first slab there is nothing to carry, and the guess falls back to FitTo(InitialCondition()). A FitTo samples its target at the nodes of the ansatz's own rule, samples it at the four surfaces of the slab as well where the fit takes them (fit_sample), fits the network to it, and takes the free part of the fitted parameters as its unknowns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.apply_guess!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{PT}, Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem{PT}, NetworkAnsatz{T, C, FT, JT, TB} where {C, FT, JT, TB<:(TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2}))}}} where {PT, T}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"check_ansatz(problem, ansatz)\n\nReject a network that the problem cannot support, when the integrator is built.\n\nThe element type must be the problem's, the network must have one output per component of the field, and the xspan must be the problem's. Each refusal is an ArgumentError that names the two values it compared, and the element type is compared first, so a pair that mismatches in both hears about the type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_ansatz_data!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"copy_ansatz_data!(cache_data, data)\n\nCopy the parameters the solver does not free from the cache of the problem's element type into the cache the solver works in.\n\nA :last_layer step frees the output weights alone and leaves the hidden layer where the fit put it, so the field of such a step and its Jacobian read the fixed parameters. A block of another element type holds its parameters at zero, and a Jacobian evaluated there would be zero with them. The free parameters come from the solver's vector on every evaluation, so they are not copied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.copy_ansatz_data!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, InitialCondition, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache, NetworkAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"evaluate!(sample, target, cache, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, problem, sol)\n\nFill sample, of size (Qt, Qx, D), with the value of target at the nodes of the ansatz's own quadrature, of which sol fixes the physical time.\n\nInitialCondition is the problem's initial condition, constant in the normalised time; PreviousSlab is the previous slab's field continued beyond its top by its time derivative there, and the initial condition before the first slab; and ExactSolution and TargetFunction are read at the physical time of each node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate_at!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab, Any, Any}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"evaluate_at!(slab, t̂, x)\n\nEvaluate the field, its two derivatives and their parameter derivative at one point into the block's scratch, without allocating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate_at!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluation_points-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkPoints}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The points of an evaluation, at which the core applies the coupling's transform of the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluation_points"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractVector{ST}, OrthogonalGreedy, NetworkAnsatz, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"fit!(w, method::OrthogonalGreedy, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, sample, cache)\n\nFit the network to a slab's sampled target, by the greedy fit of fit_network! on the workspace of the cache's block, and write the fitted atoms into w in the order L1.W, L1.b, L2.W: the two hidden weights and the bias of every selected atom, and the output weight of every component behind it. A unit the selection passed over leaves its parameters at zero.\n\nThe output weights are the ones the fit returns: the projection is orthogonal to the atoms already taken, so its coefficients are those of the atoms and not of the network, and the fit's triangular solve turns them into the output weights of a hidden layer that is already fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractVector{ST}, PINNTraining, NetworkAnsatz, NamedTuple, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"fit!(w, method::PINNTraining, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, sample, cache)\n\nFit the network to a slab's sampled target by epochs passes over the sample's points, one gradient-descent step of the method's fixed step per batch of batch consecutive points, on that batch's mean squared residual of the field against the sample's values. The points keep the order they were drawn in, and the last batch, which is the shorter one, is averaged over its own size. sample is the named tuple (; points, values, start) that fit_sample returns for the method.\n\nThe fit draws no point and evaluates no target: both are the sample's. It takes the sample's start where the free entries of w are all zero — a tanh network is stationary at the zero parameters every block begins with — and the parameters it is given anywhere else, so a :last_layer fit leaves the block's hidden layer untouched either way. The gradient is the derivative of the field with respect to each free parameter, which the block's gradient holds at every point it evaluates, so the descent needs no differentiation of its own. The batch gradient is allocated once, before the epoch loop, and each point is evaluated into the block's own scratch, so no epoch and no batch allocates.\n\nThe free parameters alone are written, and they are written into the block as well as into w, so that the evaluation of every point reads the parameters the descent has reached; a parameter the mode holds fixed keeps its value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractVector{ST}, TrainedHiddenLayer, NetworkAnsatz, NamedTuple, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"fit!(w, method::TrainedHiddenLayer, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, sample, cache)\n\nFit the hidden layer and the output layer of a :last_layer network to a slab's sampled target, by fit_trained! on the workspace of the cache's block. sample is the named tuple (; interior, faces) that fit_sample returns for the method: the target at the nodes of the ansatz's own rule, of size (Qt, Qx, D), and at the four surfaces of the slab, one row per point.\n\nThe mode is :last_layer alone: in :all mode Newton determines every parameter, so a fit that trained the hidden layer as well would hand the same weights a second owner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_atoms-Tuple{AbstractVector, NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"fit_atoms(w, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz)\n\nThe atoms a fitted parameter vector selected, as the (w₁, w₂, b) triples of its hidden units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_sample-Tuple{SlabFitMethod, Vararg{Any, 6}}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"fit_sample(method, interior, target, cache, ansatz, problem, sol)\n\nThe sample a fit method takes, from the target's interior sample at the nodes of the ansatz's own rule. A fit that takes the interior sample alone takes interior. A TrainedHiddenLayer takes the named tuple (; interior, faces), whose faces is the target at the four surfaces of the slab, one row per point, at the point sets of trace_basis(ansatz): the two surfaces in time at the space rule, then the two in space at the time rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_sample-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{PINNTraining, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, Any, Any, NetworkAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"fit_sample(::PINNTraining, interior, target, cache, ansatz, problem, sol)\n\nThe sample of a PINNTraining fit: nsamples points of the slab drawn uniformly over [0, 1] × xspan from a copy of the method's generator, and the value of the target at each of them, read by target_point! so that every target of the guess answers at the physical time of its point. The points are drawn afresh at every call and the method's own generator is never advanced, so two fits that share one method object draw the same points. Each coordinate is one Float64 draw rounded to the element type, so a fit in Float32 reads the points of the same fit in Float64.\n\nBeside them the sample carries start: one value in [-1, 1] per free parameter, drawn from the same copy of the generator after the points. A network whose parameters are all zero has a zero derivative with respect to every one of them, so fit! starts from start there and from the parameters it is given anywhere else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.fit_sample"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_residual!-Tuple{AbstractVector, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab, Integer}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"free_residual!(b, slab, nf)\n\nThe interior weak form, into the first nf entries of the solver's vector, which are the network's free parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.keep_top!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"A network carries no top row of coefficients: the guess continues its parameter vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.keep_top!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.network_functions-Tuple{SymbolicNeuralNetworks.SymbolicNeuralNetwork, Integer}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"network_functions(symbolic, D)\n\nThe two executable functions of a network of D outputs, generated once from its symbolic derivatives.\n\nfield(input, parameters) returns the 3D values [u; ∂_{t̂}u; ∂ₓu] at one point, and gradient(input, parameters) their derivative with respect to every parameter, a 3D × NP matrix. Both take the parameters as the flat vector of NeuralNetworkParameters, whose order is L1.W, L1.b, L2.W, and both read the input as [t̂, x].\n\nThe derivatives are those of SymbolicNeuralNetworks, whose Jacobian and derivative are not exported and are imported by name here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.network_functions"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.nfree-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"nfree(ansatz::NetworkAnsatz)\n\nThe number of a network's own unknowns: its free parameters, which neither the coupling nor the problem changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.nfree"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.noutputs-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The number of outputs of the network, which is the number of components of the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.noutputs"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.nparameters-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The number of the network's parameters, in the order L1.W, L1.b, L2.W.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.nparameters"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.nunknowns-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz, SlabCoupling, LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"nunknowns(ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, coupling, problem)\n\nThe number of a network's own unknowns of one slab: its free parameters, which neither the coupling nor the problem changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.nunknowns"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_evaluation-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The evaluation of the ansatz at the output grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_evaluation"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_weights-Tuple{AbstractVector, NetworkAnsatz, Integer}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"output_weights(w, ansatz::NetworkAnsatz, d)\n\nThe output weights of component d of a fitted parameter vector, one per hidden unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The rule the network evaluates its field on, which is not the trace basis'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature_points-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The normalised time nodes and the space nodes of the slab's own quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature_points"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.set_parameters!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab, AbstractVector}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"set_parameters!(slab, w)\n\nWrite a whole parameter vector of the network into the block, in the order L1.W, L1.b, L2.W.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.set_parameters!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_field!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, AbstractVector{ST}, Any, Integer, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"slab_field!(u, v, w, slab, x, bottom, nf, h)\n\nThe field of the slab that the solver's vector x describes, at the nodes of the ansatz's own quadrature and at the four surfaces of the slab: the free parameters from x, the others as the block holds them, and the field, its derivatives and their parameter derivative from the network.\n\nv is the derivative in physical time, ∂_{t̂}u / h, and the parameter derivative of v carries the same 1/h. A network fixes no bottom row, so bottom is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_weak_form!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, Vararg{Any, 4}}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"slab_weak_form!(slab, ∂u, ∂v, ∂w, h)\n\nThe interior weak form of the slab, one row per free parameter: the Euler–Lagrange residual at the nodes of the ansatz's own quadrature, weighted by the derivative of the field with respect to that parameter, which is the test function a network's rows carry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_weak_form!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.target_point!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractVector{ST}, InitialCondition, SpaceTimeGalerkinCache, NetworkAnsatz, LagrangianPDEProblem, Any, Any, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"target_point!(values, target, cache, ansatz, problem, sol, t̂, x)\n\nThe D components of target at one point of the slab, whose physical time is t₀ + h t̂.\n\nThe four targets of the guess each answer at a point, as they answer on the slab's quadrature: InitialCondition is the problem's initial condition, PreviousSlab the previous slab's field continued beyond its top, and ExactSolution and TargetFunction are read at the physical time of the point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.target_point!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_values!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{ST}, AbstractMatrix{ST}, AbstractMatrix{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab{ST, T} where T, MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkPoints, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"top_values!(u, v, w, slab, evaluation, h)\n\nThe field and its two derivatives at the top of the slab, t̂ = 1, at the points of evaluation, into u[i, d], v[i, d] and w[i, d]. ∂ₜu is the derivative in the normalised time divided by the slab's h.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_values!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_basis-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"trace_basis(ansatz::NetworkAnsatz)\n\nThe trace basis of the network's multipliers: a TensorBSplineAnsatz whose functions the coupling uses as its multipliers and on whose rules its tables are built.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_evaluation-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.NetworkSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The evaluation of the ansatz at the nodes of the trace basis' space rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_evaluation"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.xspan-Tuple{NetworkAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The space interval of the slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.xspan"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicAnsatz","page":"The ansatz families","text":"SymbolicAnsatz(expr, t, x, p; xspan, start, degree = 1, ncells = (2, 2), n = nothing)\n\nA symbolic ansatz of one space-time slab whose field is a closed-form expression.\n\nThe slab is the rectangle [0, 1] × xspan in the normalised time coordinate t̂ and the physical space coordinate x, and the field is\n\nu(t̂ x) = mathrmexpr(t̂ x p)\n\nwhere expr is a Num or a Vector{Num} of D entries, p the vector of its parameter symbols, and start the initial guess of the parameter vector on the first slab. The field's time is the slab's normalised time, so an expression in physical time is substituted before it is given here.\n\nThe constructor builds six in-place functions with Symbolics.build_function(…; expression = Val(false)): the field, its two derivatives ∂u/∂t̂ and ∂u/∂x, and the parameter Jacobian of each, a D × length(p) matrix. They are held in concrete fields, so no container of abstract type and no eval of generated code enters a step; each function compiles once, at its first call. The ansatz also holds a TensorBSplineAnsatz named trace, whose rule is the slab's quadrature and whose space basis carries the coupling's multiplier tables; the trace basis does not constrain the field.\n\nThe constructor refuses an expression whose free symbols are not t, x and the entries of p, an empty p, a start of another length than p and an xspan that does not increase, each with an ArgumentError naming the offending value. check_ansatz refuses an expression count that is not the problem's D, an element type that is not the problem's, and an xspan that is not the problem's.\n\nThe parameters are the unknowns of the slab. WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions is the one coupling the ansatz takes, and it lets the field be free at both ends of xspan, so the trace basis is built with the Free() boundary condition that coupling needs.\n\nThe integrator stores the solved top multipliers as the top_multiplier entry of each step's internal state, as it does for a spline ansatz. For a symbolic ansatz they are not unique: the Jacobian of a step has a null space in the top and side multipliers that leaves the parameters determined, so the stored values carry no meaning on their own.\n\nSymbolicAnsatz(p[1] * sin(p[2] * x - p[3] * t), t, x, p;\n    xspan = (0, 2π), start = [1, 1, 2π])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicAnsatz"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab","page":"The ansatz families","text":"SymbolicSlab{ST, T, A}\n\nThe block of a SymbolicAnsatz in the slab cache: what the ansatz keeps of one slab.\n\nparams is the parameter vector of the slab and residual its interior weak form, of one row per parameter. u, v and w hold the field and its derivatives at the nodes of the slab's quadrature, and Ju, Jv and Jw the parameter Jacobian of each there. bottom, top, left and right hold the field and its parameter Jacobian at the four boundary sets: t̂ = 0 and t̂ = 1 at the space nodes, and x = a and x = b at the time nodes. tabulation holds the tables of the slab's quadrature, and output and trace evaluate the trace basis' space basis at the output grid and at the nodes of the space rule. The six evaluators of the ansatz write into the scratch buffers ubuf to Juxbuf, so a call of slab_field!, slab_weak_form! or top_values! allocates nothing; lambda_bottom, lambda_top, mu_left and mu_right hold the multipliers' values at the nodes of the rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#GeometricIntegratorsBase.initsolver-Tuple{SimpleSolvers.Newton, SpaceTimeGalerkin{<:SymbolicAnsatz}, GeometricIntegratorsBase.CacheDict}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"initsolver(solvermethod, method, caches)\n\nThe symbolic family solves its slab with a rank-revealing linear solve.\n\nThe multiplier coupling's Jacobian is a saddle point whose constraint rows depend only on the field, so its rank is at most twice the parameter count: for the three-parameter travelling wave p₁ sin(p₂x − p₃t̂) on a trace basis of degree one and two cells, the first slab's 10 × 10 matrix has rank 6, and its four smallest singular values are at round-off. The minimum-norm step of a complete orthogonal factorization solves a consistent system exactly and drops the directions the rank does not carry, where a factorisation without rank detection loses the descent direction: started at its exact parameters, that run takes three to 29 iterations per slab with the ecosystem's LU and reaches the tolerance on its first slab alone in Float64, where the pivoted QR converges in two iterations at every slab in both precisions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricIntegratorsBase.initsolver"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{SymbolicAnsatz{T}, LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where {T, ST}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"ansatz_data(ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe ansatz's block of the slab cache, built once per integrator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data_type-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{SymbolicAnsatz{T}, LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where {T, ST}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"ansatz_data_type(ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe type of ansatz_data(ansatz, problem, ST), computed without building the block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data_type"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{PT}, Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem{PT}, SymbolicAnsatz{T}}} where {PT, T}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"check_ansatz(problem, ansatz)\n\nReject a symbolic ansatz that the problem cannot support, when the integrator is built.\n\nThe expression count must be the problem's D, the element type the problem's, and the xspan the problem's. Each refusal is an ArgumentError that names the two values it compared, and the element type is compared first, so a pair that mismatches in both hears about the type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate_at!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab{ST, T} where T, Any, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"evaluate_at!(slab, t̂, x)\n\nEvaluate the six functions of the ansatz at one point into the block's scratch: the field, its two derivatives and their three parameter Jacobians.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluate_at!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_residual!-Tuple{AbstractVector, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab, Integer}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"free_residual!(b, slab, nf)\n\nThe interior weak form, into the first nf entries of the solver's vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.keep_top!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"A symbolic ansatz carries no top row of coefficients: the guess continues its parameter vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.keep_top!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_evaluation-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The evaluation of the trace basis at the output grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_evaluation"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature-Tuple{SymbolicAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The tensor Gauss–Legendre quadrature of the slab, which the ansatz's trace basis owns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature_points-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The normalised time nodes and the space nodes of the slab's quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature_points"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_field!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab{ST, T} where T, AbstractVector{ST}, Any, Integer, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"slab_field!(u, v, w, slab, x, bottom, nf, h)\n\nThe field of the slab that the solver's vector x describes, at the nodes of the slab's quadrature and at the four boundary sets: the parameter vector from the first nf entries of x, and the field, its derivatives and their parameter Jacobians from it.\n\nv is the derivative in physical time, ∂_{t̂}u / h, and w the derivative in space; the parameter Jacobian of v carries the same 1/h. A symbolic ansatz fixes no bottom row, so bottom is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_weak_form!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab{ST, T} where T, Vararg{Any, 4}}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The interior weak form of the slab, from the density's derivatives at its nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_weak_form!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_values!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{ST}, AbstractMatrix{ST}, AbstractMatrix{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab{ST, T} where T, MultisymplecticIntegrators.PointEvaluation, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"top_values!(u, v, w, slab, evaluation, h)\n\nThe field and its two derivatives at the top of the slab, t̂ = 1, at the points of evaluation, into u[i, d], v[i, d] and w[i, d]. ∂ₜu is the derivative in the normalised time divided by the slab's h.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_values!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.total_free_coefficients-Union{Tuple{G}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{SpaceTimeGalerkin{A, C, G}, LagrangianPDEProblem}} where {A<:SymbolicAnsatz, C<:SlabCoupling, G<:SlabInitialGuess}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"total_free_coefficients(method, problem)\n\nThe size of one symbolic slab's nonlinear system: its parameters, and the coupling's unknowns after them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.total_free_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_evaluation-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.SymbolicSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The evaluation of the trace basis at the nodes of the space quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_evaluation"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.xspan-Tuple{SymbolicAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The space interval of the slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.xspan"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.PointEvaluation","page":"The ansatz families","text":"PointEvaluation{T}\n\nThe space basis at a set of points: the points, and the basis functions and their first derivatives there, a row per point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.PointEvaluation"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineAnsatz","page":"The ansatz families","text":"TensorBSplineAnsatz(; degree, ncells, xspan, x_boundary = Dirichlet(), n = nothing)\n\nA tensor B-spline ansatz of one space-time slab, on SimpleSplines.\n\nThe slab is the rectangle [0, 1] × xspan in the normalised time coordinate t̂ and the physical space coordinate x. The ansatz approximates the field by\n\nu_h(t̂ x) = sum_ij hatu_ij  B_i(t̂)  B_j(x)\n\nwith a clamped B-spline basis of degree degree[1] on ncells[1] uniform cells of [0, 1] and a B-spline basis of degree degree[2] on ncells[2] uniform cells of xspan. degree and ncells are given per direction, as a number for both or as a (time, space) tuple, so that the two directions may differ in degree and in cell count.\n\nx_boundary is the boundary condition the space basis is built with: Dirichlet() recombines the basis at both ends of xspan, so that every function vanishes there, and Free() keeps the natural condition. The ansatz carries a homogeneous Dirichlet value in x only; an inhomogeneous value is a coupling's business.\n\nn is the number of Gauss–Legendre nodes per cell in each direction, again a number or a (time, space) tuple; the default integrates the products of the basis functions and of their first derivatives exactly, which is p + 1 nodes for degree p, the count the products of the basis functions need.\n\nBoth degrees are at least 1. The slab core reads the space gradient ∂ₓu from the same space basis, and a piecewise-constant degree-0 basis carries none, so a lower degree raises ArgumentError.\n\nThe ansatz counts the degree, which is what SimpleSplines takes; a basis of order k is one of degree k - 1.\n\nansatz = TensorBSplineAnsatz(degree = 2, ncells = (1, 20), xspan = (0.0, 1.0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineAnsatz"},{"category":"type","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab","page":"The ansatz families","text":"TensorBSplineSlab{ST, T}\n\nThe block of a TensorBSplineAnsatz in the slab cache: what the ansatz keeps of one slab.\n\ncoeffs is the coefficient array of the slab, of size (Nt, Nx, D), residual its interior weak form, and top the top row of the last solved slab. tabulation holds the tables of the slab's quadrature, which the ansatz owns. output and trace evaluate the space basis at the output grid and at the nodes of the space quadrature, and top_slope holds the time functions' first derivatives at the top of the slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#GeometricBase.basis-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The tensor B-spline basis of the ansatz, on [0, 1] × xspan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricBase.basis"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#GeometricBase.degree-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The degree of the ansatz in each direction, (time, space).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricBase.degree"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2}), LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where {T, ST}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"ansatz_data(ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe ansatz's block of the slab cache, built once per integrator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data_type-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2}), LagrangianPDEProblem, Type{ST}}} where {T, ST}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"ansatz_data_type(ansatz, problem, ST)\n\nThe type of ansatz_data(ansatz, problem, ST), computed without building the block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ansatz_data_type"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{PT}, Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem{PT}, TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2})}} where {PT, T}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"check_ansatz(problem, ansatz)\n\nReject an ansatz that the problem cannot support, when the integrator is built.\n\nA tensor B-spline ansatz must be of the problem's element type and on the problem's space interval. Each refusal is an ArgumentError that names the two values it compared, and the element type is compared first, so a pair that mismatches in both hears about the type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.check_ansatz"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluation_points-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.PointEvaluation}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The points of an evaluation, at which the core applies the coupling's transform of the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.evaluation_points"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_residual!-Tuple{AbstractVector, MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab, Integer}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"free_residual!(b, slab, nf)\n\nThe rows of the weak form that the free rows carry, into the solver's layout: the rows of the test functions 1:nf, which vanish at the top of the slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.free_residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.keep_top!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"Keep the top row of the solved slab, which the coupling and the guess of the next slab read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.keep_top!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.nspace_functions-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The number of basis functions in the space direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.nspace_functions"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.ntime_functions-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The number of basis functions in the time direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.ntime_functions"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_evaluation-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The evaluation of the space basis at the output grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.output_evaluation"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The tensor Gauss–Legendre quadrature of the ansatz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature_points-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The normalised time nodes and the space nodes of the slab's quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.quadrature_points"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.scatter!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{Array{ST, 3}, AbstractVector{ST}, Any, Integer}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"scatter!(coeffs, x, bottom, nf)\n\nScatter the solver's vector into the coefficient array: the free rows, the last nf rows, from x, and the rows below them from bottom, the coupling's fixed bottom row, where nf < Nt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.scatter!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_field!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab{ST}, AbstractVector{ST}, Any, Integer, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"slab_field!(u, v, w, slab, x, bottom, nf, h)\n\nThe field of the slab that the solver's vector x describes, at the nodes of the slab's quadrature: the coefficient array from x and the coupling's fixed bottom row, and the field and its two derivatives from it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_weak_form!-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The interior weak form of the density's derivatives ∂u, ∂v, ∂w, into the block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_weak_form!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_basis-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The one-dimensional basis of the space coordinate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_quadrature_nodes-Union{Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz{T, BT} where BT<:(SimpleSplines.TensorProductBasis{T, 2})}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The nodes of the space quadrature, the points the ansatz stores its trace at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.space_quadrature_nodes"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.time_basis-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The one-dimensional basis of the normalised time coordinate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.time_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_values!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{ST}, AbstractMatrix{ST}, AbstractMatrix{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab{ST}, MultisymplecticIntegrators.PointEvaluation, Any}} where ST","page":"The ansatz families","text":"top_values!(u, v, w, slab, evaluation, h)\n\nThe field and its two derivatives at the top of the slab, t̂ = 1, at the points of evaluation, into u[i, d], v[i, d] and w[i, d]. ∂ₜu is the time derivative of the normalised coordinate divided by the slab's h, which is the factor the interior weak form carries as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.top_values!"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_evaluation-Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.TensorBSplineSlab}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The evaluation of the space basis at the nodes of the space quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.trace_evaluation"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.x_boundary-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The boundary condition the space basis is built with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.x_boundary"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.xspan-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The space interval of the slab.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.xspan"},{"category":"method","location":"ansatz/#SimpleSplines.ncells-Tuple{TensorBSplineAnsatz}","page":"The ansatz families","text":"The number of cells of the ansatz in each direction, (time, space).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimpleSplines.ncells"},{"category":"section","location":"problems/#Example-problems-and-diagnostics","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The problem layer: the Lagrangian density, the problem type and the data a slab of a problem hands out. Then the four example problems, each of which lives in its own submodule, and the figures of a run.","title":"Example problems and diagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangianDensity","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"LagrangianDensity(L, u, v, w; params = NamedTuple())\n\nThe Lagrangian density L(u, v, w; params) of a field theory in one space dimension, where v = ∂ₜu and w = ∂ₓu, together with its three partial derivatives ∂u, ∂v and ∂w.\n\nL, u, v and w are symbolic. Each of the four functions is built once by Symbolics.build_function(…; expression = Val(false)), so an evaluation is an ordinary Julia call and not a re-derivation of the expression. The names of params are the parameters of the density: every built function takes their values as further positional arguments.\n\nFields\n\nL(u, v, w, p...): the density.\n∂u(u, v, w, p...), ∂v(u, v, w, p...), ∂w(u, v, w, p...): its partial derivatives.\nnames: the names of the parameters, in the order of the built functions' arguments.\nparams: the default parameter values.\nlinear: whether the partial derivatives are affine in (u, v, w).\n\nExample\n\nusing Symbolics\n@variables u v w\nd = LagrangianDensity(v^2 / 2 - w^2 / 2, u, v, w; params = (c = 0.5,))\nd(0.0, 1.0, 0.0, (c = 0.5,))   # the density, with the parameters named\nd.∂v(0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.5)       # ∂L/∂v, with the values in argument order\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangianDensity"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivative_u","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"∂L/∂u at (u, v, w).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivative_u"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivative_v","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"∂L/∂v at (u, v, w).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivative_v"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivative_w","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"∂L/∂w at (u, v, w).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivative_w"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivatives","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The three derivatives of a density at (u, v, w), as a named tuple.\n\nA density that couples the D components of the field defines its own method. The couplings of the slab call it with NTuple{D}s for u, v and w, so a method restricted to AbstractVector does not apply; see LagrangianPDEProblem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivatives"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.is_linear_density-Tuple{LagrangianDensity}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"Whether the density's partial derivatives are affine in (u, v, w).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.is_linear_density"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.parameter_values-Union{Tuple{PT}, Tuple{NT}, Tuple{WT}, Tuple{VT}, Tuple{UT}, Tuple{LT}, Tuple{LagrangianDensity{LT, UT, VT, WT, NT, PT}, NamedTuple}} where {LT, UT, VT, WT, NT, PT<:NamedTuple}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The values of params in the order of the density's built functions.\n\nWhen the names of the default parameters are the density's names, in their order, the type of the defaults fixes that order for the compiler; otherwise each name is looked up in params.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.parameter_values"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.point_derivatives-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{LagrangianDensity, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, Integer, Any}, Tuple{LagrangianDensity, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, Integer, Any, Val{D}}} where D","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The three partial derivatives of density at node r of a boundary trace, as D-tuples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.point_derivatives"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.point_derivatives-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{ST}, Tuple{LagrangianDensity, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, Integer, Integer, Any}, Tuple{LagrangianDensity, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, AbstractArray{ST, 3}, Integer, Integer, Any, Val{D}}} where {ST, D}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The three partial derivatives of density at point (q, r) of a slab grid, as D-tuples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.point_derivatives"},{"category":"type","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangianEquation","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"LagrangianEquation{P} <: GeometricEquation\n\nThe equation of a Lagrangian field theory in one space dimension, with parameters of type P.\n\nIt carries no data of its own: the density lives in the problem, because every problem of this kind is a different density. The type exists so that a LagrangianPDEProblem has the equation type its GeometricProblem supertype needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangianEquation"},{"category":"type","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangianPDEProblem","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"LagrangianPDEProblem(density; D = 1, ics_function, bcs_function, solutions = nothing,\n                     timespan, timestep, xspan, xstep, params = density.params)\n\nA Lagrangian field theory in one space dimension on a space-time slab, with a generic element type.\n\ndensity is a Lagrangian density: a callable L(u, v, w, params) with a derivatives method returning its three partial derivatives ∂L/∂u, ∂L/∂v and ∂L/∂w. A LagrangianDensity supplies both for one component at a time; a density that couples the components of the field, such as the vector density that VectorWave.vector_density builds, supplies them for the D components of one point at once. Such a density receives the D values of u, v and w at a point as an indexable collection: the couplings of the slab pass NTuple{D}s to derivatives, and energy_error passes Vectors, or scalars where D = 1, to derivatives and to the density itself. Write its methods for any collection of length D, not for AbstractVector alone; derivatives returns the named tuple (u = …, v = …, w = …) of the D values of each partial derivative. The problem's parameters are the density's own params field where it has one, and the params keyword otherwise.\n\nics_function(x, params) returns the named tuple (u = …, v = …, w = …) of the initial data on the vector x of grid points, each entry a length(x) × D matrix whose column d holds component d. bcs_function(t, params) returns the side data of the slab at the one time t: left and right, each (u = …, v = …, w = …) of D vectors, entry d component d's value at that end. solutions is nothing where the problem has no exact solution, and otherwise the named tuple (u = …, v = …, w = …) of (t, x, params) functions that return the D components' values at a point and the component-major vector of length D * length(x) at a grid.\n\ntimespan, timestep, xspan and xstep are converted to the element type T of timespan. The functions of the problem take its parameters, never a module-level default.\n\nproblem = LagrangianPDEProblem(density;\n    ics_function = (x, p) -> (u = …, v = …, w = …),\n    bcs_function = (t, p) -> …,\n    timespan = (0.0, 1.0), timestep = 0.1, xspan = (0.0, 1.0), xstep = 0.01)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LagrangianPDEProblem"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#GeometricBase.ntime-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The number of steps a run of the problem takes, which is the number of steps the solution holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricBase.ntime"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#GeometricEquations.initial_conditions-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The initial conditions of the problem, merged with its initial time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricEquations.initial_conditions"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.components-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The number of components of the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.components"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.convert_parameters-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"convert_parameters(::Type{T}, params)\n\nThe parameters of a problem as a named tuple whose entries are all of type T, so that a closed form written with π and with the default parameters keeps the element type of its arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.convert_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.density_params-Tuple{Any}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"density_params(density)\n\nThe parameters of a density: its own params field where it has one, and nothing otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.density_params"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.field_exact-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"field_exact(f, D)\n\nThe exact function of D components, (t, x, params) returning a Vector: the D components' values at a scalar point x, and the component-major vector of length D * length(x) at a grid. A closed form that returns a number gives every component that number; one that returns a vector supplies the components' values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.field_exact"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.field_initial_data-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Any, Integer, Any}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"field_initial_data(solutions, x, params, D, t)\n\nThe initial data (u = …, v = …, w = …) of D components at time t: three (length(x), D) matrices, entry [r, d] the value of component d at the r-th point of x. Each matrix is the component-major vector that a function of solutions returns at the grid x, laid out in D columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.field_initial_data"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.field_side_data-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Integer, Any}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"field_side_data(solutions, t, params, D, xspan)\n\nThe side data of a slab at the one time t: left and right, each (u = …, v = …, w = …) of D vectors, the D-vector that a function of solutions returns at that end of xspan, entry d the exact value of component d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.field_side_data"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_start-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem, Any}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"slab_start(problem, sol)\n\nThe start time of the slab that sol ends: the solution step's time less one timestep. A solution step holds its time either as a scalar or as a GeometricBase.TimeVariable, whose single entry is the scalar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.slab_start"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.xgrid-Tuple{LagrangianPDEProblem}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"xgrid(problem)\n\nThe output grid of the problem: n = round(Int, Δx / xstep) + 1 equally spaced points from xspan[1] to xspan[2], as a range that ends on the end of the span.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.xgrid"},{"category":"module","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"Wave\n\nThe scalar wave equation ∂ₜ²u − c² ∂ₓ²u = 0 on x ∈ [0, l], with the standing-wave exact solution of the two lowest harmonics.\n\nThe Lagrangian density is L = ½v² − ½c²w², and the exact solution is\n\nu(t x) = (A₁cos(πctl) + B₁sin(πctl + π6)) sin(πxl)\n        + (A₂cos(2πctl) + B₂sin(2πctl + π6)) sin(2πxl)\n\nwith v = ∂ₜu and w = ∂ₓu its derivatives.\n\nproblem = Wave.problem(timespan = (0.0, 3.0), timestep = 0.3, xstep = 0.01)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.density-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"density(; params = default_parameters(), m = 0.0, β = 0.0)\n\nThe Lagrangian density of the wave equation, L = ½v² − ½c²w², as a LagrangianDensity whose parameter is c. A non-zero mass m or coupling β adds −½m²u² − ¼βu⁴ to the expression, with m and β fixed at construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.density"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.initial_data","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"initial_data(x::AbstractVector, params, D = 1, t = 0.0)\n\nThe initial data (u = …, v = …, w = …) at time t, each entry a length(x) × D matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.initial_data"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.problem-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"problem(; T = Float64, D = 1, params = default_parameters(), timespan = (0.0, 3.0),\n        timestep = 0.3, xspan = (0.0, 1.0), xstep = 0.01)\n\nA LagrangianPDEProblem of the wave equation, with the massless density L = ½v² − ½c²w² of which the standing wave is the exact solution. A mass or a quartic coupling is a keyword of density only, because the exact solution holds for neither. Every parameter is converted to T, so that the density and the energy of a Float32 problem are evaluated in Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.problem"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.side_data","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"side_data(t, xspan, params, D = 1)\n\nThe side data of the slab at the one time t, as field_side_data gives it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.side_data"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.solutions","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The exact solution of the wave of D components, as field_exact lifts it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Wave.solutions"},{"category":"module","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"VectorWave\n\nThe wave equation for a field u ∈ ℝᴰ on x ∈ [0, l] with zero Dirichlet data, the density\n\nL = tfrac12u_t^2 - tfracc^22u_x^2 - tfracm^22u^2 - tfracbeta4u^4\n\nthe exact solution of the uncoupled case m = β = 0, each component a standing wave of Wave with amplitudes of its own, and the angular momentum J = ∫ (u₁ ∂ₜu₂ − u₂ ∂ₜu₁) dx of the D = 2 field.\n\nL is invariant under a rotation of (u, v, w), so J is conserved; a numerical integration that loses it has lost the symmetry, which is the oracle of the vector case. The default parameters give the two components different phases, so that J is not zero.\n\nproblem = VectorWave.problem(timespan = (0.0, 1.0), timestep = 0.1, xstep = 0.05)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave"},{"category":"type","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.VectorDensity","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"VectorDensity(density, D = 2, β = 0)\n\nThe density of a component-major vector field u ∈ ℝᴰ: the scalar density summed over the D components, minus the quartic coupling ¼β|u|⁴. The scalar density carries no quartic term, so that the whole is invariant under a rotation of (u, v, w). u, v and w hold the D components' values at one point, in any indexable collection of length D: the couplings of the slab pass NTuple{D}s, and the energy diagnostics pass Vectors, or scalars where D = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.VectorDensity"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.angular_momentum-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"angular_momentum(u₁, u₂, v₁, v₂, x)\n\nJ = ∫ (u₁ v₂ − u₂ v₁) dx by the trapezoidal rule on the grid x. It is the charge of the rotation that leaves the density invariant, so an exact solution conserves it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.angular_momentum"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.component_parameters-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"component_parameters(params, i)\n\nThe parameters of Wave for component i: the shared speed c and length l, and the i-th entry of each of the amplitudes A1, A2, B1 and B2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.component_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.density-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"density(; params = default_parameters(), m = 0.0)\n\nThe scalar density of one component, ½v² − ½c²w² − ½m²u². The quartic coupling of the components is in vector_density, because ¼β|u|⁴ is not a sum over the components.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.density"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.problem-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"problem(; T = Float64, params = default_parameters(), timespan = (0.0, 3.0),\n        timestep = 0.3, xspan = (0.0, 1.0), xstep = 0.01)\n\nA LagrangianPDEProblem of D = length(params.A1) components, with zero Dirichlet data in space and the exact solution of the uncoupled case: component i is the standing wave of Wave with the parameters component_parameters(params, i). Component i is column i of the initial data, entry i of the side data, and the i-th stretch of the component-major exact solution. Every parameter is converted to T, so that the density and the energy of a Float32 problem are evaluated in Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.problem"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.vector_density-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"vector_density(; params = default_parameters(), D = 2, m = 0.0, β = 0.0)\n\nThe vector density of D components with the mass m and the quartic coupling β.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.vector_density"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivatives-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{BT}, Tuple{DT}, Tuple{MultisymplecticIntegrators.VectorWave.VectorDensity{DT, BT, D}, Vararg{Any, 4}}} where {DT, BT, D}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The derivatives of a vector density, component by component: those of the scalar density, and −β|u|²uᵢ from the quartic coupling in ∂L/∂uᵢ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivatives"},{"category":"module","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"SineGordon\n\nThe nonlinear sine-Gordon equation c ∂ₜ²u − ∂ₓ²u = −sin(u) on x ∈ [0, l], with the exact solution of the travelling kink.\n\nThe Lagrangian density is L = ½(c v² − w²) + 1 + cos(u), and the exact solution is\n\nu(t x) = 4arctan(exp(γ (x - v t))) qquad γ = 1  sqrt1 - c v²\n\nwith v = ∂ₜu and w = ∂ₓu its derivatives. γ is a function of c and the kink velocity, so the parameters are (c, velocity) and a kink exists only where c velocity² < 1.\n\nproblem = SineGordon.problem(timespan = (0.0, 10.0), timestep = 1.0, xstep = 0.01)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.density-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"density(; params = default_parameters())\n\nThe Lagrangian density L = ½(c v² − w²) + 1 + cos(u), whose parameter is c.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.density"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.lorentz_factor-Tuple{Any}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"lorentz_factor(params)\n\nγ = 1 / √(1 − c velocity²). Throws ArgumentError where the kink has no solution, which is where c velocity² ≥ 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.lorentz_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.problem-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"problem(; T = Float64, D = 1, params = default_parameters(), timespan = (0.0, 10.0),\n        timestep = 1.0, xspan = (0.0, 1.0), xstep = 0.01)\n\nA LagrangianPDEProblem of the sine-Gordon equation. Throws ArgumentError where c * velocity² ≥ 1, because the kink solution needs a velocity below the wave speed. Every parameter is converted to T, so that the density and the energy of a Float32 problem are evaluated in Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.problem"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.solutions","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The exact solution of the kink of D components, as field_exact lifts it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.SineGordon.solutions"},{"category":"module","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"LinearTransport\n\nThe linear transport equation ∂ₜu + c ∂ₓu = 0 on x ∈ [-0.5, 0.0], whose exact solution is the initial profile shifted by c t.\n\nThe Lagrangian density is the doubled field's, L = ψ (v₁ + c w₁) on D = 2: component 1 is the transported field u and component 2 the adjoint field ψ, which carries zero data. The variation in ψ gives the transport equation for u, and the variation in u the adjoint equation for ψ, so the density carries the dynamics that one field alone could not: it is linear in the velocities, its Legendre map is degenerate (p_u = ψ, p_ψ = 0), and the two components' rows carry each other's equation.\n\nThe exact solution of u is the Gaussian\n\nu(t x) = frac12sqrtpi expbig(-(x + 2 - ct)^2big)\n\nwith v = ∂ₜu and w = ∂ₓu its derivatives, and the adjoint field's exact value is zero.\n\nThe transport equation needs its inflow value at x = -0.5, which WeakInitialAndBoundaryConditions imposes and CoefficientRestriction does not, so under the restriction the error is largest at that end and the converged order is read on x ≥ -0.5 + c t.\n\nproblem = LinearTransport.problem(timespan = (0.0, 10.0), timestep = 1.0, xstep = 0.01)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport"},{"category":"type","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.TransportDensity","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"TransportDensity{P}\n\nThe doubled-field density L = ψ (v₁ + c w₁), with P the type of its parameters, of which it keeps c. The field is (u, ψ): u[1] is the transported field, u[2] the adjoint field, and v and w are the field's derivatives in time and in space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.TransportDensity"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.density-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"density(; params = default_parameters())\n\nThe Lagrangian density of the doubled field, L = ψ (v₁ + c w₁), whose parameter is the speed c.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.density"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.problem-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"problem(; T = Float64, params = default_parameters(), timespan = (0.0, 10.0),\n        timestep = 1.0, xspan = (-0.5, 0.0), xstep = 0.01)\n\nA LagrangianPDEProblem of the doubled field, D = 2 as the density needs. The default xspan is (-0.5, 0.0), where the initial profile is smooth and nonzero over the whole slab. Every parameter is converted to T, so that the density and the energy of a Float32 problem are evaluated in Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.problem"},{"category":"method","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.solutions-Tuple{}","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The exact solution of the doubled field's two components, as field_exact lifts it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.LinearTransport.solutions"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivatives-2","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"The partial derivatives of the doubled-field density, component by component: ∂L/∂u = (0, u_t + c u_x) from the adjoint field, ∂L/∂v = (ψ, 0) and ∂L/∂w = (c ψ, 0) from the transported one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.derivatives"},{"category":"module","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics\n\nFigures for the integrators of this package: a space-time plot of a solution, the energy error of several runs over time, an animation of the field, and the dispatcher that turns one archived run into the figure its kind asks for.\n\nThe methods are implemented in the MultisymplecticIntegratorsPlots extension, which loads together with Makie or a backend such as CairoMakie. The declarations here hold the API and its documentation, so that each name is documented whether or not a backend is loaded, and the extension documents no name a second time.\n\nWhy a submodule rather than three more exports\n\nplot_solution and plot_energy_error are names GeometricProblems already exports — plot_solution from its problem submodules and plot_energy_error from its own Diagnostics. Exporting them from this package's top level would make them ambiguous in any scope that also does using GeometricProblems.Wave-style imports, which is what a script integrating a problem naturally writes. The extension therefore defines Diagnostics.plot_solution, which extends the declaration rather than shadowing a name, and a caller reaches it as MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.plot_solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.animate_solution","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"animate_solution(problem, result; path, framerate = 15, title = nothing, figsize = nothing)\n\nAn animation of the field u(t, ·) over the stored steps of one run, written to path.\n\nOnly the first component of the field is animated, whatever the problem's dimension D.\n\nresult is what integrate returns and problem the problem it was integrated with. The frame at step n is the field on the problem's output grid, at the time of that step, so the animation shows the solution of the discrete problem and nothing interpolated between the steps.\n\nEach frame's axis title shows the time of its step. title = nothing draws no heading before that time, as plot_solution draws none without an archive, and an empty string draws none either; a title is drawn before the time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.animate_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.figures","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"figures(data) -> Figure\n\nThe figure one archive earns: a \"solution\" archive draws as a space-time plot, a D-component solution as one panel per component, and an \"energy\" archive as the energy error of its series.\n\ndata is a run's archive as a plain dictionary — the schema is documented in scripts/README.md. Its \"kind\" selects the shape of figure, and a kind this module cannot draw is refused rather than guessed at: two figures drawn from the wrong keys look like a result.\n\nThis is what makes a renderer a dispatcher — glob a run directory, hand each archive over, save what comes back — rather than a second registry of which experiment produces which picture, kept in step with the first by hand. Naming the file is the caller's, from the archive's \"stem\": the figure does not know where output goes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.figures"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.plot_energy_error","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"plot_energy_error(times, errors; labels = nothing, path = nothing, title = nothing, figsize = nothing)\nplot_energy_error(data::AbstractDict; kwargs...)\n\nThe relative energy error of several runs against time, on a logarithmic axis.\n\nerrors holds one series per run, in energy_error's units, and times either one time vector shared by every series or one per series. A zero error cannot be drawn on a logarithmic axis, so its point is dropped from both vectors rather than masked, which keeps the curve whole. A series with no drawable point is left out of the figure, and the legend names the series that were drawn: a legend over an empty axis claims a result that the figure does not show.\n\nThe dictionary form reads an archive whose \"kind\" is \"energy\".\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nlabels = nothing: one legend entry per series.\npath = nothing: write the figure there, as a PDF by its extension.\ntitle = nothing, figsize = nothing: as for plot_solution; the form without an archive draws no heading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.plot_energy_error"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.plot_solution","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"plot_solution(problem, result; path = nothing, title = nothing, figsize = nothing)\nplot_solution(data::AbstractDict; kwargs...)\n\nA space-time plot of one run: one heatmap panel per component of the field, the time axis running up the page and the problem's output grid across it, with the values read off the panel's colourbar.\n\nresult is what integrate returns, and problem the problem it was integrated with. The dictionary form reads the archive that scripts/archives.jl writes, whose \"kind\" is \"solution\".\n\nKeyword arguments\n\npath = nothing: write the figure there, as a PDF by its extension, beside returning it.\ntitle = nothing: the heading across the figure. nothing draws the heading of the archive, which names the run and its time step, and the form without an archive draws none; an empty string draws none either, whatever the archive holds.\nfigsize = nothing: the size of the figure, in pixels, as (width, height).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.plot_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"problems/#MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.plot_theme","page":"Example problems and diagnostics","text":"plot_theme()\n\nThe one theme of every figure of this package: the font sizes and weight, the line and marker sizes, and the colour cycle.\n\nA function rather than a value, because a Makie Theme can only exist where Makie does, and this module is loaded whether or not a backend is. The extension defines it, and applies it inside each figure method, so that no method sets a font, a line width or a colour of its own: the attributes the theme names are the theme's in every figure, whatever ambient theme the caller set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators.Diagnostics.plot_theme"},{"category":"section","location":"#MultisymplecticIntegrators","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for MultisymplecticIntegrators.\n\nMulti-symplectic schemes for Lagrangian partial differential equations in one space dimension: the Lagrangian problems with their exact solutions, the space-time Galerkin slab integrator, and the shell that drives an integrator over the slabs of the time span.\n\nnote: The package is mid-rebuild\nThe problem layer, the slab integrator and the integrator shell are in place, and the integrator families come back one at a time. A family that has not returned yet is unavailable in this version; the old stack stays at 294f023.\n\n","title":"MultisymplecticIntegrators"}]
}
